            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 42,
          },
          "title": "15.3. Hazards. Critical hazards in anchorages encompass missing hardware, cracks, dents, chips, pitting, or deformation. Caution is warranted for temporary welding, requiring inspection by a qualified person. Proper installation is crucial, aligning c...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 42,
          },
          "title": "15.4. Visual Aids. The visual aid provides a detailed list of anchors, and the typical use or purpose.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 42,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 15",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 43,
          },
          "title": "16.1. Connector. A device used to couple (connect) parts of the fall protection system together (29 CFR 1910.140(c), Definitions). See Figure 16.1. for a depiction of connectors and their capability.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 43,
          },
          "title": "16.2. Connector Hook Requirements.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 44,
          },
          "title": "16.3. Connector Failure and Hazards.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 43,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 16",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 45,
          },
          "title": "17.1. Guidelines for PFAS. IAW 29 CFR 1910.140(d)(2)(ii), personal fall arrest systems are rigged in such a manner that the employee cannot free fall more than 6 feet (1.8 m) or contact a lower level. A free fall may be more than 6 feet (1.8 m) provid...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 45,
          },
          "title": "17.2. Fall Calculation. Fall calculation must be performed before any work is performed. See Table 17.1. for a breakdown of the calculation and example.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 45,
          },
          "title": "17.3. Fall Clearance. Fall Clearance refers to the minimum vertical distance required between the worker's feet and a lower level, such as the ground or a lower working platform, to prevent contact with the lower level in case of a fall. It's crucial ...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 46,
          },
          "title": "17.4. Swing Fall. Swing fall happens when a worker moves horizontally away from the anchor point. This increases the total drop clearance needed to safely stop a fall. To prevent swing fall, it's recommended to limit it to a maximum of 4 feet (ANSI/AS...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 46,
          },
          "title": "17.5. Application. Numerous fall protection companies offer online fall calculators to help determine the most suitable equipment for organizational requirements. When opting for personal fall arrest systems (PFAS), it's essential to incorporate clear...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 46,
          },
          "title": "17.6. Hazards. Hazards associated with fall calculations include improper gear selection, calculation errors, failure to account for swing fall, and operational changes without re-evaluating the fall protection program.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 46,
          },
          "title": "17.7. Visual Aids. The visual aid connects to an OSHA Technical Manual that describes measurements for assessing fall hazards and controls.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 45,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 17",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 47,
          },
          "title": "18.1. Guardrail Systems. Guardrail Systems are erected to prevent employees from falling to lower levels. Specific guidance is described in DAFMAN 91-203, 29 CFR 1910.29(b), and 29 CFR 1926.500(b).",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 47,
          },
          "title": "18.2. Requirements. Guardrails are mandated when working at or above fall protection trigger heights and when personal fall protection is not utilized. In addition, guardrails must be free of rough and sharp edges, and the use of steel or plastic band...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 47,
          },
          "title": "18.3. Design Specifications. See Figure 18.1. Guardrail Design.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 47,
          },
          "title": "18.4. Application. Inspectors are tasked with evaluating unprotected edges leading to lower levels and must mitigate any hazards meeting the trigger height requiring fall protection. Verify that guardrails are sturdy, serviceable, and meet the specifi...",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 47,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 18",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 48,
          },
          "title": "19.1. Designated Area. A distinct portion of a walking-working surface delineated by a warning line in which employees may perform work without additional fall protection (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions). IAW 29 CFR 1910.29(d), the following requireme...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 48,
          },
          "title": "19.2. Warning Line. A barrier erected to warn employees that they are approaching an unprotected side or edge, and which designates an area in which work may take place without the use of other means of fall protection. (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 49,
          },
          "title": "19.3. Visual Aids. The visual aid connects to an OSHA Technical Manual that describes Regulation requirements and references.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 48,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 19",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 50,
          },
          "title": "20.1. Definition. Hole means a gap or open space in a floor, roof, horizontal walking-working surface, or similar surface that is at least 2 inches (5 cm) in its least dimension (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions).",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 50,
          },
          "title": "20.2. Cover. IAW 29 CFR 1926.502(i), covers for holes in floors, roofs, and other walking/working surfaces shall meet the following requirements:",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 50,
          },
          "title": "20.3. Application. Inspector shall evaluate holes in the floor or ground to see if they meet the definition of a floor opening that requires fall protection. If the hole meets the definition, then it is required to have some type of mitigation measure...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 50,
          },
          "title": "20.4. Hazards. Common areas for this hazard are raised flooring in facilities for communication lines (false flooring), catwalks, roof skylights, and construction areas that allow access through a floor opening to a lower level.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 50,
          },
          "title": "20.5. Visual Aids. The visual aid connects to an OSHA Technical Manual that describes requirements for hole covers.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 50,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 20",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 51,
          },
          "title": "21.1. Wall Opening. Each employee working on, at, above, or near wall openings (including those with chutes attached) where the outside bottom edge of the wall opening is 6 feet (1.8 m) or more above lower levels and the inside bottom edge of the wall...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 51,
          },
          "title": "21.2. Opening. A gap or open space in a wall, partition, vertical walking-working surface, or similar surface that is at least 30 inches (76 cm) high and at least 18 inches (46 cm) wide, through which an employee can fall to a lower level (29 CFR 1910...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 51,
          },
          "title": "21.3. Application. Verify the wall opening meets all three criteria and abate the hazard.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 51,
          },
          "title": "21.4. Hazards. Common areas for wall opening hazards are construction sites, loading/unloading operations at facilities, elevator shaft, weather damaged facilities, facility renovations, and aging facilities.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 51,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 21",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 52,
          },
          "title": "22.1. Stairway (Stairs). Risers and treads that connect one level with another and includes any landings and platforms in between those levels. Stairways include standard, spiral, alternating tread-type, and ship stairs (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions).",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 52,
          },
          "title": "22.2. Handrail. A rail used to provide employees with a handhold for support (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions). Effective handrails provide an adequate handhold for workers to grasp to prevent them from falling (29 CFR 1926.1052(c)(1) and 1926.1052(c)(...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 52,
          },
          "title": "22.3. Stairway and Handrail Compliance. IAW 29 CFR 1910.29(f), the employer must ensure the following:",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 52,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 22",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 53,
          },
          "title": "23.1. Introduction. Safety nets are classified as a passive fall protection system which can be installed as either a barrier to prevent a fall, or beneath the work to catch a falling worker. Safety nets are designed to decrease the fall distance, to ...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 53,
          },
          "title": "23.2. Requirements. IAW 29 CFR1926.502(c), safety net systems and their use must adhere to the following guidelines:",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 54,
          },
          "title": "23.3. Hazards. Common hazards include netting that does not meet the 6”x 6” or smaller mesh size, missing a label of proof testing, unserviceable netting, lack of prior to use inspections, and net not constructed to regulations.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 54,
          },
          "title": "23.4. Inflatable Fall Arrest Device. On March 15, 1995, Mr. Dan Dunn of 2368 Fairskies Dr., Spring Hill, Florida, received a response from OSHA regarding his inquiry about an inflatable fall arrest device. OSHA stated that the device would be evaluate...",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 53,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 23",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 55,
          },
          "title": "24.1. Scope. A Mobile Elevated Work Platform (MEWP) is a device designed to lift workers to elevated positions using platforms supported by scissors, masts, or booms.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 55,
          },
          "title": "24.2. References. Refer to DAFMAN 91-203, Chapter 16, Mobile Elevating Work Platforms and Scaffolding for additional guidance on MEWPs and scaffolding. Also, consult 29 CFR 1910 Subpart D, Walking-Working Surfaces, 29 CFR 1910 Subpart F, Powered Platf...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 55,
          },
          "title": "24.3. Requirements.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 55,
          },
          "title": "24.4. Hazards.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 55,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 24",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 56,
          },
          "title": "25.1. Definitions. Scaffold means any temporary elevated platform (supported or suspended) and its supporting structure (including points of anchorage), used for supporting employees or materials or both (29 CFR 1926.450, Definitions).",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 56,
          },
          "title": "25.2. Fall Protection Requirements for Scaffolds. 29 CFR 1926.451(g) describes the requirements for fall protection, these include:",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 56,
          },
          "title": "25.3. Training. IAW 29 CFR 1926.454(a), the employer shall have each employee who performs work while on a scaffold trained by a person qualified in the subject matter to recognize the hazards associated with the type of scaffold being used and to und...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 56,
          },
          "title": "25.4. Visual Aids. The visual aids cover OSHA's general requirements for scaffolding and scissor lifts, as well as various applications of scaffolding.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 56,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 25",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 57,
          },
          "title": "26.1. Portable Ladders. A ladder that can readily be moved or carried, and usually consists of side rails joined at intervals by steps, rungs, or cleats (29 CFR 1910.21, Definitions). Figure 26.1. illustrates portable ladder positioning requirements.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 57,
          },
          "title": "26.2. Fixed Ladders. A ladder with rails or individual rungs that is permanently attached to a structure, building, or equipment. Fixed ladders include individual-rung ladders, but not ship stairs, step bolts, or manhole steps (29 CFR 1910.21, Definit...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 58,
          },
          "title": "26.3. Ladder Safety Systems. A system designed to eliminate or reduce the possibility of falling from a ladder. A ladder safety system usually consists of a carrier, safety sleeve, lanyard, connectors, and body harness. Cages and wells are not ladder ...",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 57,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 26",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 59,
          },
          "title": "27.1. Definition. Leading edge means the edge of a floor, roof, or formwork for a floor or other walking/working surface (such as the deck) which changes location as additional floor, roof, decking, or formwork sections are placed, formed, or construc...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 59,
          },
          "title": "27.2. Requirements. IAW 29 CFR 1926.501(b)(2):",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 59,
          },
          "title": "27.3. Hazards. Common hazards are workers not following written program requirements, unauthorized workers in the leading-edge area, and the area is improperly marked off.",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 59,
          },
          "title": "27.4. Visual Aids. The visual aids include demonstrations of drop tests for Leading Edge and Non-Leading Edge PFAS.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 59,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 27",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 60,
          },
          "title": "28.1. Overview. In steel erection, a new, very narrow working surface is constantly being created as skeletal steel is erected at various heights. For many steel erectors, especially connectors, the work starts at the top level of the structure. This ...",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 60,
          },
          "title": "28.2. Requirements. IAW 29 CFR 1926.760(a):",
        },
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 60,
          },
          "title": "28.3. Visual Aids. The visual aid is a link to an OSHA eTool that helps educates employers and workers on the revised steel erection standards.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 60,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 28",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [],
          "level": 2,
          "target": {
            "kind": "internal",
            "pageNumber": 61,
          },
          "title": "29.1. Best Practices for Equipment Storage. This section outlines essential guidelines for storing fall protection equipment safely and effectively.",
        },
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 61,
      },
      "title": "Chapter 29",
    },
    {
      "children": [],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 62,
      },
      "title": "Attachment 1",
    },
    {
      "children": [],
      "level": 1,
      "target": {
        "kind": "internal",
        "pageNumber": 65,
      },
      "title": "Attachment 2",
    },
  ],
