                  "id": "th-11",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 7,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-190",
                      "pageNumber": 7,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "38 ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-9",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 7,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 7,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-7",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 2 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-191",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "QTP ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-8",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1. Trainer Responsibilities. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-192",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.1. Ensure Comprehensive Training: The trainer is responsible for providing comprehensive training  on Fall Protection concepts and procedures to all trainees, as outlined in this QTP and relevant  regulations, which encompass those described within this QTP, including those listed in Attachment 1. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-193",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.1.1. Furthermore, multiple chapters conclude with several "Visual Aid" links, offering further  elaboration and visual support on the respective topic. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-194",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.1.2. The trainer should ensure all trainees and additional trainers have access to the applicable  safety milSuite and milBook websites. This is required to access some Visual Aids. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-195",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.2. Integrate Real-World Scenarios: Customize the training program to incorporate real-world  scenarios, offering practical insights and examples to reinforce understanding. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-196",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.3. Organize Hands-On Training: Coordinate and facilitate hands-on training sessions during site  visits within the work center or at the nearest installation. This should encompass assessing the Fall  Protection program, conducting equipment usage and inspection exercises, evaluating documentation,  and identifying and mitigating hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-197",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.4. Collaborate with MAJCOM SEG: Establish communication with the respective MAJCOM  Occupational Safety (SEG) office to access relevant Fall Protection standards and guidance. This must  include access to applicable ANSI standards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-198",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.5. Demonstrate Fall Protection Equipment: Illustrate the proper functioning of fall protection  equipment either through on-site practical demonstrations, by employing examples, or through video  examples. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-199",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.1.6. Administer Knowledge Check: Conduct a Knowledge Check test for the trainee, see Attachment  2 Knowledge Check for the test and answer key. Thoroughly review any questions missed by the trainee  during the test, ensuring a comprehensive understanding. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-9",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.2. Trainee Responsibilities. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-200",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.2.1. Actively Engage in their Confined Space training program. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-201",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.2.2. Familiarize themselves with this QTP, relevant Fall Protection regulations, and forms, including  those listed on Attachment 1. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-202",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.2.3. Effectively communicate with the trainer or supervisor if any aspect of the training is unclear or  if there are questions regarding procedures. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-203",
          "pageNumber": 8,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "2.2.4. Successfully complete the Knowledge Check administered by the instructor with a score of 90%  or higher. Collaborate with the instructor to review and address any incorrectly answered questions.   ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 8,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-10",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 3 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-204",
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "FALL PROTECTION PROGRAM ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-11",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "3.1. Overview. Falls are one of the most common causes of serious work-related injuries and deaths in the  workplace. Employers must take measures in their workplaces to prevent employees from falling off  overhead platforms, elevated workstations, or into holes in the floor or walls. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-12",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "3.2. General Definitions. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-205",
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "3.2.1. Fall Protection: Means any equipment, device, or system that prevents an employee from falling  from an elevation or mitigates the effect of such a fall. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-206",
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "3.2.2. Fall Protection’s Purpose: To protect employees from serious injury or death that need to work  in elevated locations which meet the OSHA trigger heights. Hazards include working at heights, floor  openings, wall openings, and open sides of floors and platforms with access to lower levels. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-207",
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "3.2.3. Fall Prevention: Preventing workers from falling by using engineering controls (e.g., guardrails  and hole covers) or restraint systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-208",
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "3.2.4. Fall Arrest/Rescue: Preventing injury during and after a fall by using Personal Fall Arrest  Systems (PFAS) or safety nets and having an effective rescue plan in place. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-13",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "3.3. Requirements. A fall protection program is required when employees are working at heights. See  Table 3.1. for the trigger heights, which requires the implementation of a fall protection program. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-209",
          "pageNumber": 9,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Table 3.1. Fall Protection Program Trigger Heights. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-2",
