      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 14,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-20",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 4 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-301",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "FALL HAZARD SURVEY ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-21",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1. Fall Hazard Survey. IAW DAFMAN 91-203, 13.4., a fall hazard survey will be conducted in all work  areas where employees are exposed to fall hazards. The survey is designed to help employers and safety  professionals identify potential fall hazards and determine the necessary steps to mitigate or eliminate them.  This can include implementing engineering controls, such as guardrails or safety nets, as well as  administrative controls, such as training and work practices, to reduce the risk of falls. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-302",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.1. The survey team will be led by the unit competent/qualified person(s) and comprised of the work  center supervisors, authorized persons, installation Occupational Safety Office and F&ES Flight. Refer  to ANSI/ASSP Z359.2 for additional guidance about fall hazard surveys. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-303",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.2. A fall hazard survey will include: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-304",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.2.1. Identification of fall hazards and include details of each hazard such as means of access to  the fall hazards, locations of the fall hazards, tasks that create exposure to fall hazards, and hidden  fall hazards that are not always readily apparent. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-305",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.2.2. Pertinent information about the fall hazards to include showing its basic configuration.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-306",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.2.3. Identification of environmental factors that will affect the installation, use, inspection,  maintenance and dismantling of any fall protection system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-307",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.2.4. A risk assessment to determine the level of risk for each fall hazard identified.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-308",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.2.5. Appropriate abatement actions, as required by DAFI 91-202. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-309",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.2.6. Selected fall protection method(s) for each identified hazard. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-310",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.3. Competent or qualified person will prepare the fall hazard survey report and submit copies to the  unit fall protection program administrator, F&ES Flight, and installation Occupational Safety Office. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-311",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.4. Fall hazard surveys will be updated or re-accomplished when changes occur in the work center.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-312",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.1.5. When fall hazards are identified resulting from item or equipment upgrades, modification or  replacements, the DAF item managers (depot level) shall perform necessary functions (e.g., describing  planned upgrades, costs, timetables, and prioritization), for DAF equipment not meeting fall protection  standards.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-22",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2. Survey Components. The following points outline key aspects of a comprehensive Fall Hazard  Survey, covering various elements to consider. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-313",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.1. Means of access. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-314",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.2. Locations. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-315",
          "pageNumber": 15,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.3. Tasks creating fall hazard(s) exposure. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 15,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-316",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.4. Hidden hazards not readily apparent. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-317",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.5. Basic configuration information. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-318",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.6. Environmental factors affecting the installation, use, inspection, maintenance, and dismantling  of any fall protection system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-319",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.7. Risk assessment to determine the level of risk for each fall hazard identified. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-320",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.8. If applicable, RAC(s). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-321",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.9. Selected fall protection method(s) for each identified hazard. Note: If personal fall arrest systems  are selected, the calculation of all clearances will be included in the written fall protection procedures.  Employees shall not use PFAS until adequate clearances are available. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-322",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.2.10. Secondary hazards to consider are electrical, thermal, chemical, sharp edges, abrasive surfaces,  stability of structure, weather, unrelated operations, lighting, visibility, potential to hit mobile objects  below, entanglement hazard, heat/fire hazards, corrosion of equipment due to environment, not having  a two-person work policy, etc. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-23",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3. Application. Fall Hazard Surveys involve a systematic approach to identifying and mitigating fall  hazards in the workplace. Below is guidance and a breakdown of key points: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-323",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.1. Safety Professionals Evaluation. Safety professionals are tasked with assessing the placement  of anchors concerning workers to determine potential hazards. They evaluate whether anchors are  located above the head, at shoulder height, foot level, etc. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-324",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.2. Verification of Edge Protection. Safety professionals ensure that edges are adequately protected  from falls to lower levels, meeting trigger heights as per safety standards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-325",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.3. Inspection of Structure and Hardware. The integrity of the structure and hardware at the work  location is inspected to ensure they are functional and free of defects that could compromise safety. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-326",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.4. Assessment of Work Scope. Safety professionals assess the scope of work being undertaken to  determine the specific fall protection measures required for each work process. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-327",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.5. Identification of Equipment. Verification of the specific equipment needed to mitigate fall  hazards is conducted. This includes determining whether fall arrest, fall restraint, or a combination of  equipment is necessary. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-328",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.6. Implementation of Control Measures. For each identified fall hazard in the work center, a  corresponding fall protection control measure is assigned to mitigate the risk effectively. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-329",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.7. Verification of Injury Prevention Processes. Safety professionals ensure that existing processes  in place are sufficient to prevent serious injury or death in the event of a fall. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-330",
          "pageNumber": 16,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "4.3.8. Consultation for Best Practices. Safety professionals consult with SMEs, other safety  professionals, and relevant organizations to determine if best practices are being implemented and  followed. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 16,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-24",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 5 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-331",
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "FALL PROTECTION PROGRAM ASSESSMENT ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-25",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "5.1. Fall Protection Assessments. IAW DAFMAN 91-203, 13.3.3., the unit fall protection program  administrator will assess the compliance of the fall protection program requirements, with assistance of the  competent/qualified person, as needed.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-332",
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "5.1.1. Findings will be documented and corrected. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-333",
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "5.1.2. Final report will be routed to the unit commander for review and signature within 10 days of  assessment completion. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-334",
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "5.1.3. The unit fall protection program administrator will maintain a minimum of three (3) years of  completed assessments. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-335",
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "5.1.4. The installation or tenant unit’s Occupational Safety Office will complete an annual assessment  of fall protection program requirements for each unit and document results IAW DAFI 91-202. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-26",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "5.2. Assessment Checklist. Table 5.1. incorporates assessment requirements described in DAFI 91-202  and DAFMAN 91-203. It is an example that can be used by both the Occupational Safety Office and fall  protection program administrator. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-336",
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Table 5.1. Fall Protection Program Assessment Checklist ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-3",
          "pageNumber": 17,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-337",
                      "pageNumber": 17,
