          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 17,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-27",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 6 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-355",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "FALL PROTECTION AND RESCUE PROCEDURES ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-28",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.1. Overview. Fall protection and rescue procedures will be documented specifically for each workplace  and task(s) they are applied to when the use of fall protection is required (DAFMAN 91-203, 13.5).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-356",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.1.1. If a procedure can be effectively used in multiple workplaces/tasks, then the same procedure can  be used.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-357",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.1.2. Procedures shall be prepared and modified by a qualified or competent person.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-358",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.1.3. Procedures shall be reviewed every two years for currency and accuracy. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-359",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.1.4. When rescue by the F&ES Flight or contracted agencies is unavailable, then the unit shall ensure  a unit rescue team is available (DAFMAN 91-203, 13.5.3.). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-29",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2. Fall Protection Written Procedures. IAW DAFMAN 91-203, 13.5.2., procedures shall include: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-360",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.1. Purpose of the fall protection procedure. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-361",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.2. Location and photographs/diagrams of the fall hazard(s) and fall protection system setup. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-362",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.3. Appropriate standards, regulations or requirements for the task(s) conducted. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-363",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.4. Training requirements for the fall protection procedure based on the fall protection equipment  required. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-364",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.5. Fall protection system design parameters. Note: Include fall calculations based on identified  equipment and equipment design parameters, which include a detailed list of PPE and who developed  the procedure, when it was developed, system certifications and inspection logs. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-365",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.6. Equipment requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-366",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.7. Procedures documenting how to safely erect, use and dismantle the fall protection equipment. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-367",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.8. Preparatory actions to be conducted by the supervisor to ensure authorized persons know the fall  protection and rescue procedures. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-368",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.2.9. Steps to take when work is completed, e.g., clean up, storage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-30",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3. Rescue Plan. IAW DAFMAN 91-203, 13.5.2.10., a detailed rescue plan will include: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-369",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.1. Coordination with outside rescue agencies (installation F&ES Flight, contracted rescue unit, etc.)  to determine requirements to ensure prompt rescue of fallen personnel as part of preplanning. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-370",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.2. Procedures to contact the rescue agency if a fall occurs. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-371",
          "pageNumber": 18,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.3. Training on actions an authorized person can take to attempt self-rescue, when possible. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 18,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-372",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.4. Actions to be taken by the organization to rescue fallen authorized personnel, when possible. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-373",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.5. Location of rescue anchorage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-374",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.6. Equipment needed. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-375",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.7. Location of attachment to fallen employee’s harness. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-376",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.8. Specific actions to achieve successful rescue. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-377",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.9. Required training for rescuers. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-31",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.4. Understanding Suspension Trauma: Risks and Prevention. Suspension trauma, also known as  orthostatic intolerance, poses a significant hazard to workers suspended in full body harnesses after a fall. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-378",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.4.1. Suspension Trauma/Orthostatic Intolerance Hazard. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-379",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.4.1.1. Effects of Suspension: Workers suspended in full-body harnesses may lose consciousness  post-fall due to restricted blood flow, causing carbon dioxide buildup, cell damage, and  sodium/potassium imbalances, risking heart issues or blood clots. Proper recovery procedures are  vital to prevent severe harm or fatality. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-380",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.4.1.2. Contributing Factors: Several factors, including worker age, overall health, additional  injuries, and environmental stress like heat or cold, can contribute to the onset of suspension  trauma. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-381",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.4.1.3. Signs of Suspension Trauma: Within 2-3 minutes of suspension, workers may experience  symptoms such as faintness, nausea, breathlessness, dizziness, sweating, low heart rate, low blood  pressure, paleness, hot flashes, or a grey skin tone. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-382",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.4.1.4. Prevention Measures: Ensuring a snug and properly placed dorsal D-ring, centered on the  employee's back near shoulder level, maintains a safe rest angle within 30 degrees. Suspension  straps are effective in reducing the risk of suspension trauma and improving circulation for the  victim. Figure 6.1. illustrates these preventative measures.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-383",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 6.1. Safe Resting Angle and Suspension Straps ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-4",
          "pageNumber": 19,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-384",
                      "pageNumber": 19,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "30-Degree Safe Resting Angle ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-34",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 19,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-385",
                      "pageNumber": 19,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Suspension Straps ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-35",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 19,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-36",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 19,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-31",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 19,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
        {
