    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-32",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5. Example - Fall Rescue Protocols. To ensure effective response to fall incidents, rescue procedures  must be tailored to each work center, including pertinent phone numbers, necessary equipment, and  location-specific guidelines. All workers must undergo training by a competent individual on these  procedures, which should be designed to address worst-case scenarios, offering the best chance of saving a  fallen worker's life. Below is an example of fall rescue protocols. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-386",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.1. Immediately call 911 in case of a fall incident, followed by: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-387",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.2. Employ the buddy system for rescue operations. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-388",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.3. Gather essential rescue gear, including a rescue kit, extension ladder, aerial lift or scissor lift,  regular ladder, and pulley kit. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-389",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.4. Assess both location and worker-specific secondary hazards. Table 6.1. Secondary Hazards  lists examples of location and fallen worker secondary hazards.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-390",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.5. Execute the rescue plan as outlined, which may involve self-rescue, organizational rescue, or  involving fire rescue teams. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-391",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.6. Notify relevant emergency and command agencies promptly. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-392",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.7. Follow post-rescue procedures, providing necessary treatment to the fallen worker. Keep the  victim in a seated position with legs pulled to the chest until medical professionals can remove the  harness and gear at the hospital. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-393",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.8. Transfer rescue responsibilities to emergency responders upon their arrival. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-394",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.9. Post-Rescue Protocol.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-395",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.9.1. A personal fall protection system or its components subjected to impact loading must be  removed from service immediately and not used again until a competent person inspects the system  or components and determines that it is not damaged and safe for use (29 CFR 1910.140(c)(17)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-396",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.5.9.2. It is recommended that organizations regularly test their fall protection rescue plans to  identify any deficiencies. This can be integrated into various inspections or exercises under the  guidance of inspectors or unit commanders. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-397",
          "pageNumber": 20,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Table 6.1. Secondary Hazards ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
