    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-33",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 7 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-426",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "HIERARCHY OF CONTROLS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-34",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1. Initial Considerations. Fall Hazard Surveys are designed to help employers and safety professionals  identify potential fall hazards and determine the necessary steps to mitigate or eliminate them. If hazards  cannot be eliminated, they should be evaluated to help determine the appropriate fall protection systems  for the work site. Consider the following: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-427",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1.1. What is the fall distance from the working surface to the next level? ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-428",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1.2. How many workers are exposed to the hazard? ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-429",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1.3. What tasks and work areas are associated with the hazard? ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-430",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1.4. How will the worker move to perform tasks (horizontally, vertically)? ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-431",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1.5. Will the anchor placement create potential swing fall hazards, and are secure anchor points  available or easily installed nearby? ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-432",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1.6. Are there additional secondary hazards near the work area, such as overhead power lines? ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-433",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.1.7. How will prompt rescue be conducted if workers are suspended in a fall-arrest system, and what  rescue equipment is accessible in the potential fall area? ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-35",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.2. Hierarchy of Fall Protection. The Hierarchy of Fall Protection outlines a preferred order of controls  to mitigate fall hazards, starting with hazard elimination and progressing through passive fall protection,  fall restraint systems, fall arrest systems, and administrative controls. Figure 7.1. illustrates the Hierarchy  of Fall Protection Controls. Below is a description of each control: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-434",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.2.1. Hazard Elimination: The primary approach to addressing fall hazards is by eliminating them  altogether. This involves reassessing procedures, practices, locations, or equipment to eradicate  exposure to the fall hazard. For instance, relocating HVAC equipment from the roof edge to the ground  or an equipment room exemplifies hazard elimination. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-435",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.2.2. Passive Fall Protection: Passive protection, such as guardrails and hole covers, creates physical  barriers to prevent falls. This method is considered safer than relying solely on PPE due to reduced  potential for errors. Although initial costs may be higher, passive protection often proves more cost- effective in the long run compared to ongoing PPE expenses. However, its necessity depends on the  frequency and duration of exposure to fall hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-436",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.2.3. Fall Restraint Systems: Fall restraint systems prevent falls by restricting a worker's movement  within safe boundaries. Despite being less commonly mentioned in regulations, they are favored over  fall arrest systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-437",
          "pageNumber": 21,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.2.4. Fall Arrest Systems: Fall arrest systems are designed to stop a fall once it occurs, within  acceptable force and clearance parameters. However, they carry higher risks as they require stopping  the falling worker with acceptable force and preventing contact with surrounding structures or the  ground. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 21,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-438",
          "pageNumber": 22,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "7.2.5. Administrative Controls: Administrative controls, such as safety monitors or warning lines, aim  to increase worker awareness of fall hazards. However, they are considered the least preferred method  as they lack physical or positive protection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-439",
          "pageNumber": 22,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 7.1. Hierarchy of Fall Protection Controls. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": "HORIZONTAL LIFELINE AND FALL PROTECTION SYSTEMS",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-8",
          "imageId": "img-58-0",
          "pageNumber": 22,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 22,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-36",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 8 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-440",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "TYPES OF FALL PROTECTION ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-37",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.1. Types of Fall Protection. (DAFMAN 91-203, 13.2.4.1.) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-441",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.1.1. Passive Fall Protection. Passive fall protection, such as guardrails, work stands and platforms  (aircraft maintenance stands, e.g., B-1, B-4, B-5), nets, ladder cages and other devices can prevent an  employee from falling but are not directly connected to the employee. If passive fall protection is not  feasible, active fall protection is the preferred option.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-442",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.1.2. Active Fall Protection. Active fall protection devices include, but are not limited to, ladder  climbing devices and personal fall arrest, positioning, and restraint systems (requiring employees to  wear a harness and attach to an anchorage or lifeline).   ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-443",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.1.2.1. Positioning and Restraint Systems. Fall restraint/positioning systems restrict an employee’s  range of movement preventing the individual from falling. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-444",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.1.2.2. PFAS allow an employee to fall, but limits arresting loads to generally safe levels and  prevents the employee from hitting the next level. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-445",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.2.3. The four active fall protection systems are described in Table 8.1. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-446",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Table 8.1. Active Fall Protection Systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-6",
          "pageNumber": 23,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-447",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Systems ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-48",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-448",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Application ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-49",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-449",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Personal Fall Arrest  System (PFAS) ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-450",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "29 CFR  ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-451",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "1926.502(d) ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-50",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-452",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Anchorages. According to OSHA, “anchorage means a secure point of  attachment for equipment such as lifelines, lanyards, or deceleration devices.”  Examples include I-beams and tie off straps, which must be able to support a  minimum of 5,000 pounds per worker or at least twice the expected load of a  person falling 6 feet. ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-50",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-453",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Body harness. Body harnesses must secure an employee in a way that would  minimize the arresting force of a fall to 1,800 pounds or less. It must be capable  of supporting 310 pounds of combined tool and body weight. ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-51",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-454",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Components. These include connectors, lanyards, deceleration devices, and self- retracting lifelines. ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-52",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-455",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Suspension System ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-456",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "29 CFR  ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-457",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "1926.452(o) ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-53",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-458",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Suspension systems are commonly used by painters and window washers, as  they allow employees to lift and lower themselves along a vertical surface  while working hands-free. A fall arrest system must still be used alongside a  suspension system. ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-51",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-459",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Positioning System  ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-460",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "29 CFR  ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-461",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "1926.502(e) ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-54",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-462",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Also called work-positioning equipment, a positioning system is a body belt or  body harness system that allows a worker to be supported on an elevated  vertical surface, such as a wall, windowsill, or ladder, and work with both  hands free. ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-52",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-463",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Retrieval System ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-464",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "29 CFR  1926.502(d)(20) ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-55",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-465",
                      "pageNumber": 23,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "OSHA requires that “the employer shall provide for prompt rescue of  employees in the event of a fall or shall assure that employees are able to  rescue themselves.” Retrieving a worker who has fallen is essential to a fall  protection plan. Although OSHA does not provide specific instructions as to  how retrieval should be accomplished, it does outline some guidelines that  should govern a rescue plan. ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-53",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 23,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 23,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-38",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 24,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.2. Fall Protection Exceptions. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-466",
          "pageNumber": 24,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.2.1. General Industry. “When employers are inspecting, investigating, or assessing workplace  conditions or work to be performed prior to the start of work or after all work has been completed. This  exemption does not apply when fall protection systems or equipment meeting the requirements of 29  CFR 1910.29 have been installed and are available for workers to use for pre-work and post-work  inspections, investigations, or assessments” (29 CFR 1910.28(a)(2)(ii)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-467",
          "pageNumber": 24,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.2.2. Construction. Does not apply when employees are inspecting, investigating, or assessing  workplace conditions prior to the actual start of construction work or after all construction work has  been completed (29 CFR 1926.500(a)(1)).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-468",
          "pageNumber": 24,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "8.2.3. Research the task being accomplished, type of work, and applicable standards to decide if a task  is exempt. If ever in doubt, contact the next higher safety authority in your chain of command. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 24,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-39",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 9 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-469",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "PERSONAL FALL PROTECTION SYSTEMS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-40",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.1. Personal Fall Protection Systems. IAW 29 CFR 1910.140, This section establishes performance,  care, and use criteria for all personal fall protection systems. The employer must ensure that each personal  fall protection system used to comply with this part must meet the requirements of this section. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-41",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2. General Requirements. IAW 29 CFR 1910.140(c), the employer must ensure that personal fall  protection systems meet the following requirements.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-470",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.1. Connectors must be of durable steel or equivalent materials. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-471",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.2. Connectors must be corrosion-resistant and smooth to prevent system damage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-472",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.3. Each employee using Vertical Lifelines (VLLs) must have their own. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-473",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.4. Lanyards and VLLs must withstand a minimum breaking strength of 5,000 pounds (22.2 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-474",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.5. Self-retracting lifelines and lanyards limiting free fall to 2 feet (0.61 m) must handle a minimum  tensile load of 3,000 pounds (13.3 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-475",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.6. A competent person must inspect each lanyard or lifeline knot before use. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-476",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.7. D-rings, snaphooks, and carabiners must support at least 5,000 pounds (22.2 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-477",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.8. D-rings, snaphooks, and carabiners must pass a proof test of 3,600 pounds (16 kN) without  damage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-478",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.9. Snaphooks and carabiners must be automatic locking, requiring two consecutive movements to  open. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-479",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.10. Horizontal lifelines must be supervised by a qualified person and part of a personal fall arrest  system with a safety factor of at least two. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-480",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.11. Anchorages for fall protection must be separate from those for suspending employees or  platforms. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-481",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.12. Anchorages must support 5,000 pounds (22.2 kN) for each employee with a safety factor of  two. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-482",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.13. Travel restraint lines must withstand a tensile load of 5,000 pounds (22.2 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-483",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.14. Lifelines must not be natural fiber and polypropylene rope must have UV protection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-484",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.15. Fall protection systems must be used only for employee safety. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-485",
          "pageNumber": 25,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.16. Systems subjected to impact must be inspected and approved by a competent person before  reuse. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 25,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-486",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.17. Systems must be inspected for damage before each use and defective components replaced. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-487",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.18. Components must be compatible with connectors used. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-488",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.19. Equipment must be protected from damage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-489",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.20. Prompt rescue must be provided in case of a fall. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-490",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.2.21. Harness attachment points must be at the center of the back or pre-sternal if free fall is limited  to 2 feet (0.6 m). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-42",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3. Personal Fall Arrest System (PFAS). A system used to arrest an employee in a fall from a walking- working surface. It consists of a body harness, anchorage, and connector. The means of connection may  include a lanyard, deceleration device, lifeline, or a suitable combination of these (29 CFR 1910.140(b),  Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-491",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.1. System Performance Criteria. IAW 29 CFR 1910.140(d)(1), the employer must ensure that  personal fall arrest systems: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-492",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.1.1. Limit the maximum arresting force on the employee to 1,800 pounds (8 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-493",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.1.2. Bring the employee to a complete stop and limit the maximum deceleration distance the  employee travels to 3.5 feet (1.1 m). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-494",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.1.3. Have sufficient strength to withstand twice the potential impact energy of the employee  free falling a distance of 6 feet (1.8 m), or the free fall distance permitted by the system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-495",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "6.3.1.4. Sustain the employee within the system/strap configuration without contacting the  employee's neck and chin area. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-496",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.1.5. If a personal fall arrest system, meeting the criteria in Appendix D of 29 CFR 1910.140, is  used by an employee weighing less than 310 pounds (140 kg), it complies with 29 CFR  1910.140(d)(1)(i) through (iii). For employees weighing 310 pounds (140 kg) or more, if the criteria  in Appendix D are appropriately adjusted by the employer, the system also complies with 29 CFR  1910.140(d)(1)(i) through (iii). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-497",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.2. System use criteria. IAW 29 CFR 1910.140(d)(2), The employer must ensure that: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-498",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.2.1. On any horizontal lifeline that may become a VLL, the device used to connect to the  horizontal lifeline is capable of locking in both directions on the lifeline. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-499",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.2.2. Personal fall arrest systems are rigged in such a manner that the employee cannot free fall  more than 6 feet (1.8 m) or contact a lower level. A free fall may be more than 6 feet (1.8 m)  provided the employer can demonstrate the manufacturer designed the system to allow a free fall  of more than 6 feet and tested the system to ensure a maximum arresting force of 1,800 pounds (8  kN) is not exceeded. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-500",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.3. Body Belts. Body belts are prohibited as part of a PFAS. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-501",
          "pageNumber": 26,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4. PFAS Equipment Inspections and Maintenance. (DAFMAN 91-203, 13.8.) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 26,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-502",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4.1. PFAS users shall comply with TO 00-25-245, Operations Instructions - Testing and  Inspection procedures for Personnel Safety and Rescue Equipment, and manufacturer’s  instructions for inspection, maintenance, cleaning, and storage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-503",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4.2. Authorized person(s) shall inspect PFAS prior to use. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-504",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4.3. Supervisors shall ensure all PFAS components receive a thorough inspection at least  quarterly. This inspection shall be documented and maintained for at least one year. Exception:  Components of a PFAS that have been professionally installed and certified will be inspected in  accordance with manufacturer’s instructions, at the interval recommended by the manufacturer,  and by a person qualified to perform the inspection. Inspection shall be documented and  maintained. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-505",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4.4. Supervisors shall maintain manufacturer’s instructions and performance testing  information for PFAS used by their employees. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-506",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4.5. If defects or damage to equipment or inadequate maintenance of equipment is found, the  equipment shall be immediately tagged unserviceable and removed from service. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-507",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4.6. Unserviceable PFAS will be destroyed to prevent further use. Inspections shall be  documented in accordance with TO 00-25-245 or manufacturer’s instructions, whichever is more  restrictive. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-508",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.3.4.7. For overseas locations, the use of anchorage points is authorized when the host country  provides documentation of applicable testing and inspections. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-43",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4. Positioning Systems (Work-Positioning System).  A system of equipment and connectors that,  when used with a body harness or body belt, allows an employee to be supported on an elevated vertical  surface, such as a wall or windowsill, and work with both hands free. Positioning systems also are called  "positioning system devices" and "work-positioning equipment" (29 CFR 1910.140(b), Definitions). See  Figure 9.1. for examples of Positioning Systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-509",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1. System performance requirements. IAW 29 CFR 1910.140(e)(1), the employer must ensure that  each positioning system meets the following requirements: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-510",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1.1. All positioning systems, excluding window cleaners' positioning systems, shall  demonstrate the capability to endure, without failure, a drop test involving a 4-foot (1.2-m) drop of  a 250-pound (113-kg) weight. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-511",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1.2. Devices shall be rigged such that an employee cannot free fall more than 2 feet (.6 m). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-512",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1.3. Devices shall be secured to an anchorage capable of supporting at least twice the potential  impact load of an employee's fall or 3,000 pounds (13.3 kN), whichever is greater. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-513",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1.4. Connectors shall be drop forged, pressed, or formed steel, or made of equivalent materials.  Connectors shall have a corrosion-resistant finish, and all surfaces and edges shall be smooth to  prevent damage to interfacing parts of this system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-514",
          "pageNumber": 27,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1.5. Unless the snaphook is a locking type and designed for the following connections,  snaphooks shall not be engaged directly to webbing, rope, or wire rope, to each other, to a D-ring  to which another snaphook or other connector is attached, to a horizontal lifeline. Or, to any object ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 27,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-515",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "which is incompatibly shaped or dimensioned in relation to the snaphook such that unintentional  disengagement could occur by the connected object being able to depress the snaphook keeper and  release itself. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-516",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1.6. Positioning device systems shall be inspected prior to each use for wear, damage, and other  deterioration, and defective components shall be removed from service. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-517",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.1.7. Body belts, harnesses, and components shall be used only for employee protection (as part  of a personal fall arrest system or positioning device system) and not to hoist materials. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-518",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.2. Window Cleaners' Positioning Systems. A system which consists of a window cleaner's belt  secured to window anchors (29 CFR 1910.140(b), Definitions). IAW 29 CFR 1910.140(e)(1)(ii), all  window cleaners' positioning systems must: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-519",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.2.1. Withstand, without failure, a drop test involving a 6-foot (1.8-m) drop of a 250-pound (113- kg) weight. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-520",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.2.2. Limit the initial arresting force on the falling employee to not more than 2,000 pounds (8.9  kN), with a duration not exceeding 2 milliseconds, and subsequent arresting forces to not more than  1,000 pounds (4.5 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-521",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.3. Compliance Verification. Positioning systems, inclusive of window cleaners' positioning  systems, complying with the test methods and procedures outlined in 29 CFR 1910.140, Appendix D  shall be deemed in accordance with applicable regulations. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-522",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.4. Lineman's Body Belt and Pole Strap Systems. Lineman's body belt and pole strap systems shall  undergo the following tests: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-523",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.4.1. A dielectric test of 819.7 volts, AC, per centimeter (25,000 volts per foot) for 3 minutes  without visible deterioration. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-524",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.4.2. A leakage test of 98.4 volts, AC, per centimeter (3,000 volts per foot) with a leakage current  of no more than 1 mA. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-525",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.4.3. A flammability test in accordance with 29 CFR 1910.140, Table I-7. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-526",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5. System Use Criteria for Window Cleaners' Positioning Systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-527",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.1. Window cleaners' belts shall be designed and constructed to ensure belt terminals do not  pass through their fastenings on the belt or harness if a terminal becomes dislodged from the  window anchor. The length of the runner from terminal tip to terminal tip is 8 feet (2.44 m) or less. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-528",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.2. Window anchors to which belts are affixed shall be installed in the side frames or mullions  of the window at a height not less than 42 inches (106.7 cm) and not more than 51 inches (129.5  cm) above the windowsill. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-529",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.3. Each window anchor shall be capable of supporting a minimum load of 6,000 pounds (26.5  kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-530",
          "pageNumber": 28,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.4. The utilization of installed window anchors for any purpose other than attaching the  window cleaner's belt is prohibited. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 28,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-531",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.5. Damaged or deteriorated fastenings or supports of a window anchor shall result in either  removal of the anchor or detachment of the anchor head to render it unusable. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-532",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.6. Rope exhibiting wear or deterioration affecting its strength shall not be utilized. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-533",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.7. Both terminals of the window cleaner's belt shall be attached to separate window anchors  during any cleaning operation. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-534",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.8. No employee shall work on a windowsill or ledge under conditions of snow, ice, or any  other slippery substance, or on a weakened or rotted sill or ledge. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-535",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.9. No employee shall work on a window sill or ledge unless the window sill or ledge is a  minimum of 4 inches (10 cm) wide and slopes no more than 15 degrees below horizontal or the 4- inch minimum width of the window sill or ledge is increased by 0.4 inches (1 cm) for every degree  the sill or ledge slopes beyond 15 degrees, up to a maximum slope of 30 degrees. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-536",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.10. Before climbing through the window opening, an employee shall attach at least one belt  terminal to a window anchor and keep at least one terminal attached until completely back inside  the window opening. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-537",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.11. Employees shall travel from one window to another by returning inside the window  opening and repeating the belt terminal attachment procedure at each window. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-538",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.4.5.12. An employee using a window cleaner's positioning system may travel from one window  to another while outside of the building, provided: At least one belt terminal is always attached to  a window anchor. The horizontal distance between window anchors does not exceed 4 feet (1.2 m).  This distance may be increased up to 6 feet (1.8 m) horizontally if the windowsill or ledge is at  least 1 foot (0.31 m) wide and the slope is less than 5 degrees. The sill or ledge between windows  is continuous. The width of the windowsill or ledge in front of the mullions is at least 6 inches (15.2  cm) wide. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-539",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 9.1. Positioning System Examples ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-9",
          "imageId": "img-73-0",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-44",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.5. Additional Personal Fall Protection Systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-540",
          "pageNumber": 29,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.5.1. Personal Fall Protection System. A system (including all components) an employer uses to  provide protection from falling or to safely arrest an employee's fall if one occurs. Examples of personal  fall protection systems include personal fall arrest systems, positioning systems, and travel restraint  systems (29 CFR 1910.140(b), Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 29,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-541",
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.5.2. Rope Descent System. A suspension system that allows an employee to descend in a controlled  manner and, as needed, stop at any point during the descent. A rope descent system usually consists of  a roof anchorage, support rope, a descent device, carabiner(s) or shackle(s), and a chair (seatboard). A  rope descent system also is called controlled descent equipment or apparatus. Rope descent systems do  not include industrial rope access systems. (29 CFR 1910.21, Definitions).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-542",
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.5.2.1. Rope Access System is a means of working at height using synthetic fiber kernmantle ropes  and associated equipment to access, be supported, and used as a means of egress from a workplace  that provides 100% fall protection coverage (ANSI-ASSP Z459.1 Safety Requirements for Rope  Access System). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-543",
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.5.3. Travel Restraint Systems. A combination of an anchorage, anchorage connector, lanyard (or other  means of connection), and body support that an employer uses to eliminate the possibility of an  employee going over the edge of a walking-working surface (29 CFR 1910.140(b), Definitions). See  Figure 9.2. for an illustration of a Travel Restraint System set-up.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-544",
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 9.2. Travel Restraint System. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-10",
          "imageId": "img-76-0",
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-45",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "9.6. Visual Aids. The visual aid shows the Guardian Fall Protection Anchor, Body Wear, and Connector.  It illustrates the key components of a fall protection system used for worker safety at heights. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-545",
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. Guardian Fall Protection Anchor, Body Wear, Connector ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-546",
          "pageNumber": 30,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57434) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 30,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-46",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 10 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-547",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "LIFELINES ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-47",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.1. Lifeline Definition. A component of a personal fall protection system consisting of a flexible line  for connection to an anchorage at one end so as to hang vertically (Vertical Lifeline), or for connection to  anchorages at both ends so as to stretch horizontally (horizontal lifeline) and serves as a means for  connecting other components of the system to the anchorage (29 CFR 1910.140(b), Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-48",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.2. Vertical Lifeline (VLL). (ANSI/ASSP Z359.1-2020)  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-548",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.2.1. VLLs connect the employee’s full-body harness to the anchorage/anchorage connector as part  of the personal fall protection system. VLLs allow the employee to travel down and/or up the lifeline  while conducting tasks. Determination of whether an employee may be able to travel in both directions  will be determined by the type of fall arrestor used on the system which is used to stop a fall while on  a VLL. Figure 10.1. illustrates a standard setup. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-549",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.2.2. When VLLs are used, each employee must be attached to a separate lifeline. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-550",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.2.3. VLLs must have a minimum breaking strength of 5,000 pounds (22.2 kN) (29 CFR  1910.140(c)(4)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-551",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.2.4. A competent person or qualified person must inspect each knot in a lanyard or VLL to ensure  that it meets the requirements of paragraphs 29 CFR 1910.140(c)(4) and 1910.140(c)(5) before any  employee uses the lanyard or lifeline. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-49",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3. Horizontal Lifeline. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-552",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.1. Horizontal Lifelines shall be designed, installed, and used, under the supervision of a qualified  person, as part of a complete personal fall arrest system, which maintains a safety factor of at least two  (29 CFR 1926.502(d)(8)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-553",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.2. Horizontal Lifeline Application and Requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-554",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.2.1. Application. The system must be designed for more than one employee if more than one  is tied onto the lifeline (ANSI/ASSE Z359.6-2016). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-555",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.2.2. Requirements. A full body harness. Connection “O” ring for Self-Retracting Lanyard  (SRL), lanyard, shock absorbing lanyard, or device authorized by the qualified person to be utilized  with the system. Two anchor points meeting requirements, which accommodate the number of  workers intended to utilize the horizontal lifeline. The line must run from each anchor point  installed by a qualified person. The line is typically made of kernmantle, webbing, or metal cabling.  If a kit is used, reference the manufacturer’s instructions. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-556",
          "pageNumber": 31,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.3. On suspended scaffolds or similar work platforms with Horizontal Lifelines which may become  VLLs, the devices used to connect to a Horizontal Lifeline shall be capable of locking in both directions  on the lifeline (29 CFR 1926.502(d)(7)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 31,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-557",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.4. Horizontal Lifelines are typically installed on worksites where multiple workers are performing  work from a great height or when free movement over an area is needed without having to constantly  disconnect and reattach to different anchor points.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-558",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.5. Horizontal Lifelines typically consists of a wire rope, or a synthetic fiber rope anchored and  tensioned between two anchor points with intermediate anchor points on longer lengths. Workers attach  themselves to the horizontal lifeline’s slider or shuttle using a variety of fall arrest equipment such as a  body harness, lanyard, and connectors. See Figure 10.1. for an illustration of typical set-up and Figure  10.1. for additional examples.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-559",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.6. Horizontal Lifelines can be used for both fall arrest or fall restraint depending on how they are  used. When used as a travel restraint system, such as on a roof top, protection can be achieved by  ensuring that the lanyard does not allow the worker to reach the edge of the roof.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-560",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.7. Roof Horizontal Lifeline systems are engineered to prevent falls when workers are accessing,  inspecting, or repairing roofs. There are multiple things you need to consider when assessing roof  lifelines, such as structure types, building strength, roofing material, and anchor point location. The  building height and work required also determine the length of the roof lifeline system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-561",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.8. Horizontal Lifeline Sag. The amount of sag in the line and/or its elasticity can magnify the fall  arrest forces at the anchorages. They must be taken into consideration when designing the fall arrest  system. The greater the sag, the greater the forces during a fall arrest. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-562",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.3.9. Inspection. When inspecting a horizontal lifeline system, consult the qualified person who  designed and supervised the installation and use of the system.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-50",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.4. Rigid Rail System. A Rigid Rail System is an overhead track and trolley fall arrest system, paired  with a self-retracting lanyard for workers, and provides fall arrest solutions in environments that require  very short arrest distances. Rigid rail systems are perfect for warehouses, factories, aircraft hangars,  transportation depots, and other facilities with various ceiling heights. See Figure 10.1. for an example.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-51",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.5. Portable Gantry. Portable Gantry systems are ideal for applications where a permanent solution is  not possible. It allows for single or multiple users to complete their required tasks and can then be moved  out of the way when not needed. See Figure 10.1. for an example. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-52",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "10.6. Visual Aids. The visual aid demonstrates a horizontal lifeline setup. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-563",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57568",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Horizontal Lifeline Demonstration",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-564",
          "pageNumber": 32,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57568) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 32,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
