    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-53",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 11 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-571",
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "ROOF REQUIREMENTS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-54",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "11.1. Definitions and Requirements.   ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-572",
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "11.1.1. Low-Slope Roof. Any roof that is flat or less than or equal to a ratio of 4-foot in 12-foot (vertical  to horizontal) (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-573",
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "11.1.2. Steep Roofs. Any roof greater than a 4-foot in 12-foot slope roof. Each employee on a steep  roof with unprotected sides and edges 6 feet (1.8 m) or more above lower levels shall be protected from  falling by guardrail systems with toe boards, safety net systems, or PFAS (29 CFR 1926.501(b)(11)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-55",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "11.2. Roof Incline. Both pitch and slope indicate the incline of a roof, expressed as a proportion of the  vertical to the horizontal. Figure 11.1. illustrates how roof incline is calculated and identified.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-574",
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 11.1. Roof Nomenclature and Slope ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-17",
          "imageId": "img-94-0",
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": "Chart

Description automatically generated",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-18",
          "imageId": "img-92-0",
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-56",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 34,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "11.3. Roof Parapets. Roof Parets is a protective wall along the edge of a roof are common on modern  low sloped roofs; however, older facilities will have many fall hazards associated with their roof tops.  Keep a keen eye for active-duty military, Air Force civilians, and Space Force civilians that are  conducting operations on roof tops. Make sure they are utilizing fall protection equipment, are properly  tied off, and have a means of rescue if a fall occurs. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 34,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-57",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 12 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-575",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "BODY HARNESS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-58",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.1. Body Harness. Straps that secure about the employee in a manner to distribute the fall arrest forces  over the thighs, pelvis, waist, chest, and shoulders, with a means for attaching the harness to other ",
            },
            {
              "text": "components of a personal fall protection system (29 CFR 1910.140(b), Definitions). Figure 12.1 illustrates ",
            },
            {
              "text": "two different type of body harness.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-576",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 12.1. Body Harness ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-19",
          "imageId": "img-97-0",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": "A close-up of a robot

Description automatically generated with low confidence",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-20",
          "imageId": "img-98-0",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-59",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.2. Webbing Material.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-577",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.2.1. Nylon is Soft and flexible and stretches with enough weight added to it. It absorbs water and  can get moldy. Wet nylon is weaker.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-578",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.2.2. Polyester is more durable and rigid than nylon. It resists water absorption. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-579",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.2.3. Specialty harnesses such as ones that are flame retardant, chemical resistant, anti-static, high  heat resistant, abrasion and cut resistant can be selected based on job specifications. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-60",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.3. D-Ring Location and Application. In fall protection systems, various D-ring attachment points play  crucial roles in ensuring the safety of personnel. Figure 12.2. illustrates D-ring locations and application. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-580",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.3.1. Dorsal D-rings are at the center of the employee’s back near shoulder level (29 CFR  1910.140(c)(20)). Air Force and Space Force personnel commonly use this D-ring as the primary  connection point for fall arrest and fall restraint applications. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-581",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.3.2. Sternal D-rings are uncommonly used in Air Force and Space Force application. Sternal is used  primarily for specialized rescue, ladder safety systems, tower ascending/descending, and restraint  applications. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-582",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.3.3. Hip/Side D-rings are used for work positioning applications. These are uncommonly used by  Air Force and Space Force personnel. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-583",
          "pageNumber": 35,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.3.4. Shoulder D-rings are used for confined space rescue applications. These are uncommonly used  by Air Force and Space Force personnel.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 35,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-584",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 12.2. D-Ring Location and Application ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": "A screenshot of a video game

Description automatically generated",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-21",
          "imageId": "img-102-0",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-61",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.4. Inspection. Equipment must be inspected prior to use. The inspection shall begin at one end of the  harness working towards the other end. Never start in the middle as this increases the chance to miss defects.  If any discrepancies are noted, DO NOT use the equipment, and remove from service. Figure 12.3  illustrates items to look for and inspect.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-585",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.4.1. Impact Indicator. Harnesses may have two types of impact indicators. The first is integrated  into the harness D-ring, deploying upon a fall while retaining the D-ring. The second type involves a  fold-over style indicator where the rear vertical webbing tears upon impact. These indicators signal to  users and competent persons that the harness has been exposed to fall forces and should be retired. The  integrity of the impact indicator is crucial, with variations depending on equipment style. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-586",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.4.2. Check to ensure labels and tags are in place, legible, and within lifetime requirements per the  manufacturer’s instructions.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-587",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.4.3. Check webbing for signs of excessive wear, cuts, ultraviolet damage, and burn marks. Check  stitching for missing or lose threads. Check hardware for signs of pitting, corrosion, gouges, damage,  and metal discoloration. D-rings, snap hooks, and carabiners must be capable of sustaining a minimum  tensile load of 5,000 lbs. (22.2 kN).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-588",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.4.4. Heat will start to degrade nylon and polyester webbing at approximately 180F (melting point  approximately 450F). Check equipment for burns, warped material, and scorch marks. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-589",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.4.5. Writing on the webbing of the harness is prohibited due to the unknown side effects that the  marker will have on the webbing material. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-590",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 12.3. Inspections ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-8",
          "pageNumber": 36,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-591",
                      "pageNumber": 36,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Body Harness D Ring ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
