    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-62",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.5. Sizing. The proper size of the body harness will be determined by the height and weight of the  individual worker. There will be a sizing chart included in the manufacturer’s instruction as well as online.  These charts shall be consulted prior to purchasing body harnesses and prior to selecting one to perform  work to determine the appropriate size for each worker on location. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-63",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.6. Donning Procedures. To don the harness properly, begin by inspecting it thoroughly for any signs  of damage or wear. Position the back D-ring between your shoulder blades to ensure optimal safety. Next,  buckle up the leg straps securely, followed by buckling up the front of the harness. Once all buckles are  secured, adjust the harness so that it fits snugly around your body while ensuring the D-ring remains in the  correct position. This meticulous process guarantees that the harness is correctly fitted and ready for use,  providing maximum protection during any activity. See Figure 12.6. for an illustration of each step. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-594",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 12.6. Steps to Harness Donning ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-9",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-595",
                      "pageNumber": 37,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Inspect ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-69",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-596",
                      "pageNumber": 37,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Position back D- ring between shoulder blades",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-70",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-597",
                      "pageNumber": 37,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Buckle up legs ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-71",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-598",
                      "pageNumber": 37,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Buckle up Front ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-72",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-599",
                      "pageNumber": 37,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Adjust so the harness fits snuggly, and D- ring remains in the correct position",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-73",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-74",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-61",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-62",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-63",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-64",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 37,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-25",
          "imageId": "img-109-0",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-26",
          "imageId": "img-111-0",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-27",
          "imageId": "img-113-0",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-28",
          "imageId": "img-115-0",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-29",
          "imageId": "img-117-0",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-64",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "12.7. Visual Aids. The visual aids showcase the inspection of SALA fall protection equipment, and the  process of donning a body harness for fall protection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-600",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57862",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Fall Protection Equipment Inspection SALA",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-601",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57862) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-602",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57432",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Fall Protection, Donning a Body Harness",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-603",
          "pageNumber": 37,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57432)   ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 37,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-65",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 13 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-604",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "LANYARDS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-66",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.1. Lanyards. Within fall protection, lanyard means a flexible line of rope, wire rope, or strap that  generally has a connector at each end for connecting the body belt or body harness to a deceleration device,  lifeline, or anchorage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-67",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.2. Common Types.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-605",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.2.1. Shock Absorbing Lanyards. Utilized in fall arrest or fall restraint application. Shock absorbers  require approximately 450 lbs. of force to deploy the shock absorber (based on manufacturer’s  instructions from several industry leading fall protection companies). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-606",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.2.2. Non-Shock Absorbing Lanyards (Positioning Lanyards). Designed to keep the worker in place  with a fixed length. These lanyards are used only in fall restraint application. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-607",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 13.1. Shock Absorbing versus Non-Shock Absorbing Lanyard ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-30",
          "imageId": "img-121-0",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-68",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.3 Application. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-608",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.3.1. The worker including tools/equipment must not exceed 310 lbs. on a standard shock absorbing  lanyard. Workers/tools/equipment exceeding 311-420 lbs. will use Heavy Duty fall protection  equipment (heavy duty lanyards have a freefall distance of 12’ and must be tied off overhead). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-609",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.3.2. Maximum freefall is 6 feet for a standard shock absorbing lanyard, or the freefall distance  permitted by the system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-610",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.3.3. Max arresting force applied to the body is 1800 lbs. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-611",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.3.4. Lanyards are web or cable of lengths 6 feet or less. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-612",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.3.5. Lanyard maximum deceleration distance is 3.5 feet (42”). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-69",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.4. Inspection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-613",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.4.1. Prior to use, inspection begins at one end of the lanyard, rope, or rope grab working towards the  other end and shall never start in the middle as this increases the chance to miss defects. If any  discrepancies are noted, DO NOT use the equipment, and remove from service: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-614",
          "pageNumber": 38,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.4.2. The impact indicator must be intact. Indicators will vary by equipment style.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 38,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-615",
          "pageNumber": 39,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.4.3. Labels/Tags must be in place, legible, and within lifetime of date of first use. If no date of first  use, refer to manufactured date (present, legible, current).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-616",
          "pageNumber": 39,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.4.4. Check webbing for signs of excessive wear, cuts, and burns. Check stitches for missing or loose  threads. Check hardware for signs of damage, rust, or corrosion. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-617",
          "pageNumber": 39,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.4.5. Check to ensure connectors operate freely and secure properly.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-618",
          "pageNumber": 39,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "13.4.6. D-rings, snap hooks, and carabiners must be capable of sustaining a minimum tensile load of  5,000 lbs. (22.2 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 39,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-70",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 14 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-619",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "SELF-RETRACTING DEVICES ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-71",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.1. Self-Retracting Lifeline/Lanyard. A deceleration device containing a drum-wound line that can be  slowly extracted from, or retracted onto, the drum under slight tension during normal movement by the  employee. At the onset of a fall, the device automatically locks the drum and arrests the fall (29 CFR  1910.140(b)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-620",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.1.1. Self-Retracting Devices come in various forms, including Self-Retracting Lanyards (SRLs),  Self-Retracting Lanyards with integral rescue capability (SRL-Rs), and Self-Retracting Lanyards,  personal (SRL-Ps). SRLs are commonly used in aircraft hangars or vehicle maintenance facilities with  rigid rail overhead fall protection system tracks. On the other hand, SRL-Ps are smaller and portable,  designed to be worn directly on the user’s full-body harness and attached to an approved anchor  (ANSI/ASSP Z359.14-2021). Figure 14.1. and Figure 14.2. show SRL-P and SRL on a rigid rail fall  protection system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-621",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.1.2. Deceleration distance refers to the vertical distance between a worker's harness attachment  point at the onset of a fall and their final resting position. It's crucial to consider deceleration distances  when selecting fall protection equipment. SRLs play a vital role in minimizing fall distances and  reducing the total force applied to the body by engaging immediately upon a fall. These devices are  equipped with deceleration brake systems, allowing for effective fall protection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-10",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-622",
                      "pageNumber": 40,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Figure 14.1. SRL-P ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-75",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 40,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-623",
                      "pageNumber": 40,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Figure 14.2. SRL - ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-624",
                      "pageNumber": 40,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "On a rigid rail fall protection system ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-76",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 40,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-77",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 40,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-65",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 40,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-31",
          "imageId": "img-126-0",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-32",
          "imageId": "img-128-0",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-72",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.2. Self-Retracting Devices Classes. Self-Retracting Devices are categorized into two classes: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-625",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.2.1. Class 1:  Class 1 devices are suitable for use only when anchored above the user’s head and  when the maximum freefall distance is restricted to 2 feet. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-626",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.2.2. Class 2: Class 2 devices can be utilized when an overhead anchor point is unavailable or  impractical, and there's a possibility of contact with a structural edge. The maximum allowable freefall  distance should be limited to 6 feet. It's important to note that using a foot-level anchor point poses a  higher risk of serious injury. Hence, users should opt for the highest possible anchor point and resort to  attaching at foot level only as a last resort. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-73",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.3. Application.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-627",
          "pageNumber": 40,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.3.1. Adhere to weight limitations: Maximum weight allowed when using standard gear is 310 lbs.  Heavier loads ranging from 311-420 lbs. require heavy-duty equipment. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 40,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-628",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.3.2. Maximum force during fall arrest: Should not exceed 1800 lbs. Class 1 and 2 SRLs typically  apply around 1350 lbs. of force. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-629",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.3.3. Maximum arresting distance: Set at 42 inches. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-630",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.3.4. Lanyards: Available in various lengths and materials such as webbing or cable. Play a critical  role in fall protection systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-74",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.4. Inspection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-631",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.4.1. Begin inspecting the self-retracting lanyard from one end to the other to ensure thoroughness  and reduce the risk of overlooking defects. Alongside a detailed examination following manufacturer  instructions, a ground-level visual check of the anchor, drum, and lanyard suffices. Any noted  discrepancies warrant immediate removal from service. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-632",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.4.2. Ensure the impact indicator, such as snap hook or energy absorber, remains intact. Refer to  Figure 14.3 for indications: Green denotes serviceability, while Red signifies unserviceability. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-633",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 14.3. Impact indicators  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-634",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Green/Serviceable                 Red/Unserviceable ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": "A picture containing metalware, metal, hook

Description automatically generated",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-33",
          "imageId": "img-132-0",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "p-635",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.4.3. Verify that all labels and tags are present, legible, and adhere to guidelines, ensuring they  provide accurate information about the equipment's status and specifications. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-636",
          "pageNumber": 41,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "14.4.4. Thoroughly inspect all components, including webbing, cables, hardware, and housing, for signs  of wear, damage, or deterioration. Ensure connectors operate smoothly, and test functionality by  checking for smooth payout and retraction of webbing or cable with adequate tension. Conduct a quick  pull test to verify brake functionality. The housing must have a minimum breaking strength of 5,000  lbs. (22.2 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 41,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-75",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 15 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-637",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "ANCHORAGE AND ANCHOR POINT ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-76",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.1. Anchorage. A secure point of attachment for equipment such as lifelines, lanyards, or deceleration  devices (29 CFR 1910.140(c), Definitions).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-638",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.1.1. Anchorages used to attach to personal fall protection equipment must be independent of any  anchorage used to suspend employees or platforms on which employees work. Anchorages used to  attach to personal fall protection equipment on mobile work platforms on powered industrial trucks  must be attached to an overhead member of the platform, at a point located above and near the center  of the platform (29 CFR 1910.140(c)(12)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-639",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.1.2. IAW 29 CFR1910.140(c)(13), anchorages, except window cleaners' belt anchors, must be  capable of supporting at least 5,000 pounds (22.2 kN) for each employee attached; or designed,  installed, and used, under the supervision of qualified person, as part of a complete personal fall  protection system that maintains a safety factor of at least two. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-640",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.1.2.1. Anchors rated below 5000 lbs. with a safety factor of 2 must be certified in writing by a  qualified person. The certification document must be maintained by the organization. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-641",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.1.2.2. For anchorage certified below 5000 lbs. a safety professional must verify the qualified  person meets OSHA requirements and review the certification of the qualified person. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-642",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.1.2.3. If both verifications are valid, a certified anchor point rated below 5000 lbs. can only be  used for the operations it is certified for. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-77",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.2. Inspection. During equipment inspection, it is crucial to thoroughly examine all hardware, checking  for any signs of damage, rust, or corrosion. If any defects are noted, remove from service, and notify a  competent person. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-643",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.2.1. Check the impact indication (if a feature) or look for signs of impact loading. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-644",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.2.2. Swages should be inspected for wear or slippage, while ensuring that all moving components  operate freely and are securely fastened. Verify that the anchorage can support the designed or  appropriate loads and installed correctly. Lastly, confirm that all attachment fasteners are in serviceable  condition to ensure the safety and reliability of the equipment. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-645",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.2.3. Ensure that the anchorage location is easily accessible. Ensure no hardware is missing. There  must be no restricted visibility of the anchor while utilizing it. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-78",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.3. Hazards. Critical hazards in anchorages encompass missing hardware, cracks, dents, chips, pitting,  or deformation. Caution is warranted for temporary welding, requiring inspection by a qualified person.  Proper installation is crucial, aligning connectors with anchor point D-rings to prevent unintended shear  forces, with swiveling D-rings recommended for effective hazard mitigation. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-79",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "15.4. Visual Aids. The visual aid provides a detailed list of anchors, and the typical use or purpose. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-646",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#appc",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Examples of Anchors",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-647",
          "pageNumber": 42,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#appc) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 42,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-80",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 16 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-648",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "CONNECTORS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-81",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.1. Connector. A device used to couple (connect) parts of the fall protection system together (29 CFR  1910.140(c), Definitions). See Figure 16.1. for a depiction of connectors and their capability.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-649",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.1.1. Connectors must be drop forged, pressed, or formed steel, or made of equivalent materials (29  CFR 1910.140(c)(1)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-650",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.1.1. Connectors must have a corrosion-resistant finish, and all surfaces and edges must be smooth  to prevent damage to interfacing parts of the system (29 CFR 1910.140(c)(2)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-651",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 16.1 Types of Connectors and Compatibility ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-34",
          "imageId": "img-141-0",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-35",
          "imageId": "img-139-0",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-82",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2. Connector Hook Requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-652",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2.1. The gate must be rated at ≥3600 lbs. for both frontal and side loading. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-653",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2.2. Hooks should be sized appropriately to prevent unintentional disengagement when connected  to equipment. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-654",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2.3. They must be self-closing and self-locking for enhanced safety. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-655",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2.4. Proof-testing to 3600 lbs. is necessary. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-656",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2.5. Hooks should have a minimum tensile strength of ≥5000 lbs. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-657",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2.6. They must be smooth and free of sharp or rough edges to prevent damage. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-658",
          "pageNumber": 43,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.2.7. Double action gates are required, meaning the gate must undergo two separate consecutive  movements to open. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 43,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-83",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.3. Connector Failure and Hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-659",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.3.1. Forced Roll Out occurs when excessive force causes the gate of the connector to fail or roll out.  To prevent this, the gate must be rated to handle a minimum of ≥3600 lbs. of force. Check the hardware  for the gate's load rating, and if it's not specified, remove it from service immediately. See Figure 16.2  for an example of what forced roll out looks like. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-660",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.3.2. Side Loading occurs when the load is unintentionally applied to the connector from the side or  at an angle, which can weaken the connector's strength when used incorrectly. To prevent side loading,  connectors should always be used in line with anchor points and with connectors of similar size that  are compatible with the anchor point. Figure 16.3. shows a rebar hook that is too large for the temporary  anchor it is attached to creating a side loading hazard. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-661",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "16.3.3. Rope grabs are connectors equipped with a deceleration device that moves along a lifeline. They  engage the lifeline automatically through friction to stop an employee's fall. It's essential to refer to the  manufacturer's instructions to ensure proper usage of rope grabs for tasks requiring fall protection.  However, it's worth noting that Air Force and Space Force personnel rarely use these devices. Figure  16.4. shows a rope grab.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-662",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "          Figure 16.2. Forced Roll Out Damage.                        Figure 16.3. Side Loading Hazard. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": "A picture containing outdoor, water, ship, boat

Description automatically generated",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-36",
          "imageId": "img-148-0",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "altText": "A picture containing person, outdoor

Description automatically generated",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-37",
          "imageId": "img-145-0",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "p-663",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 16.5. Rope Grab ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-38",
          "imageId": "img-146-0",
          "pageNumber": 44,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 44,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-84",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 17 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-664",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "FALL CALCULATIONS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-85",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.1. Guidelines for PFAS. IAW 29 CFR 1910.140(d)(2)(ii), personal fall arrest systems are rigged in such  a manner that the employee cannot free fall more than 6 feet (1.8 m) or contact a lower level. A free fall  may be more than 6 feet (1.8 m) provided the employer can demonstrate the manufacturer designed the  system to allow a free fall of more than 6 feet and tested the system to ensure a maximum arresting force  of 1,800 pounds (8 kN) is not exceeded. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-665",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.1.1. A competent person and/or qualified person shall determine fall clearance minimum heights,  determine the proper equipment, anchor points, and mitigate secondary hazards.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-86",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.2. Fall Calculation. Fall calculation must be performed before any work is performed. See Table 17.1.  for a breakdown of the calculation and example.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-666",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.2.1. Maximum Arresting Force (MAF) refers to the highest force exerted on the body when stopping  a fall. It's determined by multiplying the weight of the worker and equipment by the distance of the fall. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-667",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.2.2. The MAF limit for a fallen worker's body is 1800 lbs. Exceeding this force without proper  deceleration or shock absorption could result in severe injury or death. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-668",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Table 17.1. Fall Calculation ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-11",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-669",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Fall Calculation  ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-670",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": " W x D = Total Force to Body ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-78",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-671",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "W ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-79",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-672",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Body Weight + Equipment ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-66",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-673",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "D ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-80",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-674",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Freefall distance ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-67",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-675",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Total arresting force applied to the body ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-81",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-676",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "W x D = total force applied to body ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-68",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-677",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Example: A 200 lbs. worker with 6 lbs. of tools who falls 6 feet can generate fall forces of 1,236 lbs.  across a person’s body. ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-678",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "W: 200 lbs. worker + 6 lbs. of tools = 206 lbs. total ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-679",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "D: 6-foot freefall distance ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "p-680",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "206 lbs. X 6 feet = 1236 lbs. of total arresting force ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-82",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-87",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.3. Fall Clearance. Fall Clearance refers to the minimum vertical distance required between the worker's  feet and a lower level, such as the ground or a lower working platform, to prevent contact with the lower  level in case of a fall. It's crucial that the calculated fall clearance of a fall protection system is always less  than the available clearance between the worker's level and any lower platform. To determine the fall  clearance, certain factors need to be known. See Table 17.2. and Figure 17.3. for a breakdown of the Fall  Clearance calculation. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-681",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Table 17.2. Fall Clearance Calculation ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-12",
          "pageNumber": 45,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-682",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Fall Clearance Calculation ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-83",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-683",
                      "pageNumber": 45,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Fall Clearance = Length of the Lanyard (Freefall Distance) + Deceleration Distance + Height of  Worker + Safety Factor + Swing Fall (As Required) ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-84",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 45,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 45,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-684",
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 17.3. Determining Fall Clearance ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": "Calculating Total Fall Distance For Best Fall Arrest Safety",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-39",
          "imageId": "img-153-0",
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-88",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.4. Swing Fall. Swing fall happens when a worker moves horizontally away from the anchor point. This  increases the total drop clearance needed to safely stop a fall. To prevent swing fall, it's recommended to  limit it to a maximum of 4 feet (ANSI/ASSE Z359.6-2016). When anchors are not aligned with the worker  or when the job requires moving away from the anchor, swing fall should be factored into the calculation.  Neglecting to consider swing fall could lead to severe injury or even death. See Figure 17.4. for a  breakdown of Swing Fall Clearance calculation. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-685",
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 17.4. Swing Fall Clearance ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-40",
          "imageId": "img-154-0",
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-89",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.5. Application. Numerous fall protection companies offer online fall calculators to help determine the  most suitable equipment for organizational requirements. When opting for personal fall arrest systems  (PFAS), it's essential to incorporate clearance calculations into the written fall protection procedures.  Employees must refrain from using PFAS until sufficient clearances are confirmed. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-90",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.6. Hazards. Hazards associated with fall calculations include improper gear selection, calculation errors,  failure to account for swing fall, and operational changes without re-evaluating the fall protection program. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-686",
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.6.1. To address inadequate clearance options, solutions may involve using a shorter lanyard, raising  the anchorage point, opting for a self-retracting lanyard, considering alternatives to eliminate the need  for active fall protection, or implementing a restraint system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-91",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "17.7. Visual Aids. The visual aid connects to an OSHA Technical Manual that describes measurements for  assessing fall hazards and controls. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-687",
          "pageNumber": 46,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#measurements",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "OSHA General Requirements for Fall Calculations",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 46,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-688",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#measurements) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-92",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 18 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-689",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "GUARDRAILS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-93",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.1. Guardrail Systems. Guardrail Systems are erected to prevent employees from falling to lower levels. ",
            },
            {
              "text": "Specific guidance is described in DAFMAN 91-203, 29 CFR 1910.29(b), and 29 CFR 1926.500(b). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-94",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.2. Requirements. Guardrails are mandated when working at or above fall protection trigger heights and  when personal fall protection is not utilized. In addition, guardrails must be free of rough and sharp edges,  and the use of steel or plastic banding is prohibited. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-95",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.3. Design Specifications. See Figure 18.1. Guardrail Design. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-690",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.3.1. The top rail should be 42” + 3” tall with a strength rating of 200 lbs. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-691",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.3.2. The mid rail should be half the height of the top rail and support a load of 150 lbs. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-692",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.3.3. The toe board must have a minimum height of 3.5” and a maximum gap underneath of 0.25”, with a load rating of 50 lbs.",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-693",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.3.4. Vertical posts should be spaced no more than 8 feet apart. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-694",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 18.1. Guardrail Design ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-41",
          "imageId": "img-158-0",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-96",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.4. Application. Inspectors are tasked with evaluating unprotected edges leading to lower levels and must ",
            },
            {
              "text": "mitigate any hazards meeting the trigger height requiring fall protection. Verify that guardrails are sturdy, ",
            },
            {
              "text": "serviceable, and meet the specified height and load requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-695",
          "pageNumber": 47,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "18.4.1. Common guardrail hazards include inadequate height of the top rail or missing mid rail, absence of the toe board, guardrail instability or deflection when pressure is applied and degradation due to corrosion or weather damage.",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 47,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-97",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 19 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-696",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "DESIGNATED AREAS AND WARNING LINE SYSTEMS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-98",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1. Designated Area. A distinct portion of a walking-working surface delineated by a warning line in  which employees may perform work without additional fall protection (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions).  IAW 29 CFR 1910.29(d), the following requirements must be met: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-697",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.1. Employees must remain within the designated area during work operations. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-698",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.2. The perimeter of the designated area must be marked with a warning line, which can be a rope,  wire, tape, or chain. This line must meet the following criteria: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-699",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.2.1. Have a minimum breaking strength of 200 pounds (0.89 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-700",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.2.2. Be positioned with its lowest point, accounting for sag, between 34 inches (86 cm) and  39 inches (99 cm) above the walking-working surface. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-701",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.2.3. Be supported to prevent slack from adjacent sections, ensuring it remains within the  specified height range. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-702",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.2.4. Be clearly visible from a distance of 25 feet (7.6 m) and from anywhere within the  designated area. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-703",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.2.5. Be erected as close to the work area as possible. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-704",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.2.6. Be installed no less than 6 feet (1.8 m) from the roof edge for temporary and infrequent  work or no less than 15 feet (4.6 m) for other work. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-705",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.3. When mobile mechanical equipment is used for temporary and infrequent work within a  designated area, the warning line must be positioned: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-706",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.3.1. Not less than 6 feet (1.8 m) from the unprotected side or edge parallel to the equipment's  operating direction. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-707",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.1.3.2. Not less than 10 feet (3 m) from the unprotected side or edge perpendicular to the  equipment's operating direction. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-99",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2. Warning Line. A barrier erected to warn employees that they are approaching an unprotected side  or edge, and which designates an area in which work may take place without the use of other means of  fall protection. (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions). See Figure 19.1. for an illustration of a Warning Line  setup. IAW 29 CFR 1926.502(f), the following requirements must be met: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "altText": "Diagram

Description automatically generated",
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-42",
          "imageId": "img-162-0",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "p-708",
          "pageNumber": 48,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 19.1. Warning Line System ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 48,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-709",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.1. Erection of Warning Lines: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-710",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.1.1. Warning lines must enclose all sides of the roof work area. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-711",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.1.2. When mechanical equipment is not in use, Warning lines must be set up at least 6 feet  (1.8 m) from the roof edge. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-712",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.1.3. When mechanical equipment is in use, Warning lines must be placed at least 6 feet (1.8  m) from the roof edge parallel to the equipment's operation direction. Additionally, Warning lines  must also be positioned at least 10 feet (3.1 m) from the roof edge perpendicular to the equipment's  operation direction. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-713",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.2. Connection to Work Area: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-714",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.2.1. Points of access, material handling areas, storage areas, and hoisting areas must be linked  to the work area by an access path bordered by two warning lines. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-715",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.2.2. When access paths are not in use, barricades of equal strength and height to warning lines  must block the path's intersection with the warning line around the work area. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-716",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.3. Construction of Warning Lines. Warning lines consist of ropes, wires, or chains supported by  stanchions, set up as follows: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-717",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.3.1. Flagged at intervals of not more than 6 feet (1.8 m) with high-visibility material. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-718",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.3.2. Positioned with the lowest point (including sag) no less than 34 inches (0.9 m) from the  walking/working surface and the highest point no more than 39 inches (1.0 m) from the  walking/working surface. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-719",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.3.3. Stanchions must resist a horizontal force of at least 16 pounds (71 N) applied 30 inches  (.8 m) above the walking/working surface, perpendicular to the warning line, without tipping over. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-720",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.3.4. Ropes, wires, or chains must have a minimum tensile strength of 500 pounds (2.22 kN)  and be capable of supporting loads as specified. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-721",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.3.5. Lines must be attached to each stanchion to prevent slack when pulled. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-722",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.4. Employees are not permitted between a roof edge and a warning line unless performing roofing  work in that area. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-723",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.2.5. Mechanical equipment on roofs can only be used or stored in areas protected by a warning line  system, guardrail system, or personal fall arrest system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-100",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "19.3. Visual Aids. The visual aid connects to an OSHA Technical Manual that describes Regulation  requirements and references. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-724",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Regulation requirements and references on Warning Line Systems, Safety Monitoring  Systems, and Controlled Access Zones (CAZs).",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-725",
          "pageNumber": 49,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 49,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-101",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 20 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-726",
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "HOLE COVERS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-102",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.1. Definition. Hole means a gap or open space in a floor, roof, horizontal walking-working surface, or  similar surface that is at least 2 inches (5 cm) in its least dimension (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-103",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.2. Cover. IAW 29 CFR 1926.502(i), covers for holes in floors, roofs, and other walking/working  surfaces shall meet the following requirements: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-727",
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.2.1. Covers located in roadways and vehicular aisles shall be capable of supporting, without failure,  at least twice the maximum axle load of the largest vehicle expected to cross over the cover. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-728",
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.2.2. All other covers shall be capable of supporting, without failure, at least twice the weight of  employees, equipment, and materials that may be imposed on the cover at any one time. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-729",
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.2.3. All covers shall be secured when installed to prevent accidental displacement by the wind,  equipment, or employees. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-730",
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.2.4. All covers shall be color coded, or they shall be marked with the word "HOLE" or "COVER"  to provide warning of the hazard. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-104",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.3. Application. Inspector shall evaluate holes in the floor or ground to see if they meet the definition of  a floor opening that requires fall protection. If the hole meets the definition, then it is required to have some  type of mitigation measure in place. If a hole cover is not adequate, the use of a guardrail system, fence, or  barricade will mitigate the hazard where foot traffic occurs, especially if the hole is not readily seen due to  vegetation or other visual barriers. (Examples include, pits, ditches, trenches, or shafts) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-105",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.4. Hazards. Common areas for this hazard are raised flooring in facilities for communication lines (false  flooring), catwalks, roof skylights, and construction areas that allow access through a floor opening to a  lower level. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-106",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "20.5. Visual Aids. The visual aid connects to an OSHA Technical Manual that describes requirements for  hole covers.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-731",
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#hole",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "OSHA Hole Cover General Requirements",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-732",
          "pageNumber": 50,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#hole) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 50,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-107",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 21 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-733",
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "WALL OPENINGS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-108",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "21.1. Wall Opening. Each employee working on, at, above, or near wall openings (including those with  chutes attached) where the outside bottom edge of the wall opening is 6 feet (1.8 m) or more above lower  levels and the inside bottom edge of the wall opening is less than 39 inches (1.0 m) above the  walking/working surface, shall be protected from falling by the use of a guardrail system, a safety net  system, or a personal fall arrest system (29 CFR 1926.501(b)(14)). See Figure 21.1. for a depiction of  OSHA requirements.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-109",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "21.2. Opening. A gap or open space in a wall, partition, vertical walking-working surface, or similar surface  that is at least 30 inches (76 cm) high and at least 18 inches (46 cm) wide, through which an employee can  fall to a lower level (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-734",
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 21.1. Wall Opening Criteria ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-43",
          "imageId": "img-172-0",
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "table-13",
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-735",
                      "pageNumber": 51,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Ref #1 ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-85",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 51,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-736",
                      "pageNumber": 51,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "The outside bottom edge of the wall opening MUST be 6 feet or  more above a lower level ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-86",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 51,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-737",
                      "pageNumber": 51,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Ref #2 ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-87",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 51,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-738",
                      "pageNumber": 51,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "The inside bottom edge of the wall opening is less than 39” from  the floor ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-69",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 51,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-739",
                      "pageNumber": 51,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Ref #3 ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-88",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 51,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-740",
                      "pageNumber": 51,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Greater than or equal to 30” high and 18” or wider in any wall or  partition ",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "td-70",
                  "isHeader": false,
                  "pageNumber": 51,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "type": "table",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-110",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "21.3. Application. Verify the wall opening meets all three criteria and abate the hazard. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-111",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 51,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "21.4. Hazards. Common areas for wall opening hazards are construction sites, loading/unloading  operations at facilities, elevator shaft, weather damaged facilities, facility renovations, and aging facilities. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 51,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-112",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 22 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-741",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "STAIRWAYS AND HANDRAILS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-113",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.1. Stairway (Stairs). Risers and treads that connect one level with another and includes any landings  and platforms in between those levels. Stairways include standard, spiral, alternating tread-type, and ship  stairs (29 CFR 1910.21(b), Definitions).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-742",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.1.1. A Stairrail system is a vertical barrier that runs along the unprotected side edge of a stairway to  prevent workers from falling to lower levels. The top surface of the stairrail system may be used as the  handrail (29 CFR 1926.1050(b); 29 CFR 1926.1052(c)(7)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-114",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.2. Handrail. A rail used to provide employees with a handhold for support (29 CFR 1910.21(b),  Definitions). Effective handrails provide an adequate handhold for workers to grasp to prevent them from  falling (29 CFR 1926.1052(c)(1) and 1926.1052(c)(9)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-115",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3. Stairway and Handrail Compliance. IAW 29 CFR 1910.29(f), the employer must ensure the  following: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-743",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.1. Handrails are positioned between 30 inches (76 cm) and 38 inches (97 cm) from the leading  edge of the stair tread to the top surface of the handrail. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-744",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.2. Stair rail systems' height depends on installation date: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-745",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.2.1. Installed before January 17, 2017: minimum 30 inches (76 cm) from the leading edge of  the stair tread to the top surface of the top rail.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-746",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.2.2. Installed on or after January 17, 2017: minimum 42 inches (107 cm) from the leading  edge of the stair tread to the top surface of the top rail.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-747",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.3. The top rail of a stair rail system can serve as a handrail if it meets specific height requirements  and other handrail criteria. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-748",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.4. There must be a minimum clearance of 2.25 inches (5.7 cm) between handrails and any other  object. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-749",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.5. Handrails and stair rail systems must have smooth surfaces to prevent employee injuries and  clothing snagging. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-750",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.6. No opening in a stair rail system can exceed 19 inches (48 cm) at its least dimension. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-751",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.7. Handrails should be shaped and sized to allow employees to grasp them firmly. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-752",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.8. The ends of handrails and stair rail systems should not present any projection hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-753",
          "pageNumber": 52,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "22.3.9. Handrails and top rails of stair rail systems must withstand a downward or outward force of at  least 200 pounds (890 N) applied within 2 inches (5 cm) of any point along the top edge of the rail  without failing. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 52,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-116",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 23 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-754",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "SAFETY NETS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-117",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.1. Introduction. Safety nets are classified as a passive fall protection system which can be installed as  either a barrier to prevent a fall, or beneath the work to catch a falling worker. Safety nets are designed to  decrease the fall distance, to absorb the energy of a fall, and to reduce the likelihood or seriousness of an  injury (29 CFR 1926.105; 29 CFR1926.502(c)). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-755",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.1.1. Safety nets shall be provided when workplaces are more than 25 feet above the ground or water  surface, or other surfaces where the use of ladders, scaffolds, catch platforms, temporary floors, safety  lines, or safety belts is impractical (29 CFR1926.105(a)). Figure 23.1. illustrates the net requirements  at different heights.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-118",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2. Requirements. IAW 29 CFR1926.502(c), safety net systems and their use must adhere to the  following guidelines: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-756",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.1. Safety nets should be placed as close as possible beneath the working surface, not exceeding 30  feet (9.1 m) below. On bridges, the area between the working surface and the net must be clear. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-757",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.2. The outer edge of safety nets must extend outward from the outermost projection of the work  surface, with specific minimum distances depending on the vertical distance from the working level to  the horizontal plane of the net. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-758",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.3. Sufficient clearance must exist under safety nets to prevent contact with surfaces or structures  below during an impact force equal to the drop test specified. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-759",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.4. Safety nets and installations must withstand an impact force equivalent to a drop test using a  specified weight and height. Drop tests should occur after installation, relocation, major repair, and at  6-month intervals if stationary. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-760",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.5. Defective nets must not be used. Weekly inspections for wear, damage, and deterioration are  required, with defective components promptly removed. Additionally, inspections are necessary after  any event potentially affecting net integrity. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-761",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.6. Materials, tools, or equipment fallen into safety nets must be promptly removed, preferably  before the next work shift. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-762",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.7. Safety net mesh openings must not exceed specific size limits, with mesh crossings securely  fastened to prevent enlargement. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-763",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.8. Each safety net section must have a border rope with a minimum breaking strength of 5,000  pounds (22.2 kN). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-764",
          "pageNumber": 53,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.2.9. Connections between safety net panels must be as strong as integral net components and spaced  no more than 6 inches (15 cm) apart. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 53,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-119",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 54,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.3. Hazards. Common hazards include netting that does not meet the 6”x 6” or smaller mesh size, missing  a label of proof testing, unserviceable netting, lack of prior to use inspections, and net not constructed to  regulations. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-765",
          "pageNumber": 54,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 22.1. Net Requirements for Working at Heights ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "altText": undefined,
          "caption": undefined,
          "id": "fig-44",
          "imageId": "img-179-0",
          "pageNumber": 54,
          "type": "figure",
          "unsupported": false,
        },
        {
          "id": "h-120",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 54,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "23.4. Inflatable Fall Arrest Device. On March 15, 1995, Mr. Dan Dunn of 2368 Fairskies Dr., Spring Hill,  Florida, received a response from OSHA regarding his inquiry about an inflatable fall arrest device. OSHA  stated that the device would be evaluated based on criteria similar to safety nets. If the device meets the  standards outlined in 29 CFR 1926.502(c) and effectively prevents the test weight from reaching the ground,  it could be considered compliant. However, it must be installed within 30 feet of the working surface as  specified in the net standards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 54,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-121",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 24 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-766",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "MOBILE ELEVATED WORK PLATFORM ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-122",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.1. Scope. A Mobile Elevated Work Platform (MEWP) is a device designed to lift workers to elevated  positions using platforms supported by scissors, masts, or booms. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-123",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.2. References. Refer to DAFMAN 91-203, Chapter 16, Mobile Elevating Work Platforms and  Scaffolding for additional guidance on MEWPs and scaffolding. Also, consult 29 CFR 1910 Subpart D,  Walking-Working Surfaces, 29 CFR 1910 Subpart F, Powered Platforms, Manlifts, and Vehicle-Mounted  Work Platforms, and 29 CFR 1910.27, Scaffolds and Rope Descent Systems. All fall prevention and  protection measures must be evaluated and approved by a qualified person before use. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-124",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.3. Requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-767",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.3.1. Compliance with Standards: Vehicle-mounted elevating and rotating aerial devices must meet  the requirements of ANSI/SAIA A92.2-2021, Vehicle Mounted Elevating and Rotating Aerial Devices.  Any modifications should conform to ANSI/SAIA A92.2-2021 and 29 CFR 1910.67, Vehicle-Mounted  Elevating and Rotating Work Platforms, and documented appropriately. Note that removing or  tampering with safety features is prohibited (DAFMAN 91-203, 16.5.1.).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-768",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.3.1.1. An inspector should check the equipment status paperwork (Air Force Technical Order  (AFTO) Form 244, Industrial/Support Equipment Record or equivalent) to ensure operability. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-769",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.3.2. Visual Inspection: Before each shift or day's work, visually inspect the unit and test lift controls  to ensure safe operation. Test the override feature as per ANSI/SAIA A92.2-2021 requirements and  document any discrepancies found during the inspection (DAFMAN 91-203, 16.5.5.4).   ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-770",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.3.3. Guardrail System: Platforms, excluding buckets or baskets, should have a guardrail system with  a top rail 42 inches high and at least one rail midway between the top rail and platform surface. Both  rails must withstand 300 pounds of force without failure (DAFMAN 91-203, 16.5.5.9.1.).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-771",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.3.4. Safety Harness: Occupants of buckets or platforms must wear a full-body harness and lanyard,  limiting free fall distance to six feet or less. Each lanyard should be attached to a designated anchor  point on the boom or basket (DAFMAN 91-203, 16.5.5.10.).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-125",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.4. Hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-772",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.4.1. While guardrails and buckets offer some fall protection, the catapulting tendency of mobile lift  baskets poses a risk of workers being ejected from the lift, leading to severe injury or death.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-773",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.4.2. During operation, there's a risk of workers coming into contact with live electrical components  or getting entangled in rotating or moving equipment parts. Collisions with stationary objects like  aircraft, vehicles, or poles are also potential hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-774",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.4.3. Adverse weather conditions such as high winds, rain, snow, ice, or hail can affect the stability  and safety of the lift, posing risks to workers. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-775",
          "pageNumber": 55,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "24.4.4. Equipment not properly maintained and regularly inspected may have issues like low hydraulic  pressure, worn tread, rust, or damaged controls, increasing the likelihood of accidents and malfunctions. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 55,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-126",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 25 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-776",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "SCAFFOLDING ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-127",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.1. Definitions. Scaffold means any temporary elevated platform (supported or suspended) and its  supporting structure (including points of anchorage), used for supporting employees or materials or both  (29 CFR 1926.450, Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-128",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.2. Fall Protection Requirements for Scaffolds. 29 CFR 1926.451(g) describes the requirements for  fall protection, these include: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-777",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.2.1. Fall Protection for Elevated Scaffolds: Employees on scaffolds over 10 feet above ground level  must be protected from falls. Specific types of fall protection are outlined in subsections 29 CFR  1926.451(g)(i) through 1926.451(g)(vii). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-778",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.2.2. Requirements for Scaffold Erectors and Dismantlers: Effective from September 2, 1997, a  competent person must assess the feasibility and safety of providing fall protection during scaffold  erection or dismantling. Fall protection is mandatory where feasible and does not pose additional  hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-779",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.2.3. PFAS specifications: Personal fall arrest systems must be attached to vertical or horizontal  lifelines or scaffold structural members, meeting specific anchorage and attachment criteria outlined in  subsections 29 CFR 1926.451(g)(iii) and 1926.451(g)(iv). Additionally, guardrail systems must comply  with various provisions regarding installation, height, strength, and surface properties, as detailed in  subsections 29 CFR 1926.451(g)(i) through 1926.451(g)(xv).  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-129",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.3. Training. IAW 29 CFR 1926.454(a), the employer shall have each employee who performs work  while on a scaffold trained by a person qualified in the subject matter to recognize the hazards associated  with the type of scaffold being used and to understand the procedures to control or minimize those hazards.  The training shall include the following areas, as applicable: ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-780",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.3.1. The nature of any electrical hazards, fall hazards and falling object hazards in the work area. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-781",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.3.2. The correct procedures for dealing with electrical hazards and for erecting, maintaining, and  disassembling the fall protection systems and falling object protection systems being used. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-782",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.3.3. Retraining is required where changes in the types of scaffolds, fall protection, falling object  protection, or other equipment present a hazard for which an employee has not been previously trained. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-130",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "25.4. Visual Aids. The visual aids cover OSHA's general requirements for scaffolding and scissor lifts, as  well as various applications of scaffolding. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-783",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/etools/scaffolding/supported",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "OSHA Scaffolding General Requirements",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-784",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/etools/scaffolding/supported) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-785",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/etools/scaffolding/scissor-lifts",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "OSHA Scissor Lift General Requirements",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-786",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/etools/scaffolding/scissor-lifts) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-787",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#appa",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Scaffolding Applications",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-788",
          "pageNumber": 56,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/otm/section-5-construction-operations/chapter-4#appa) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 56,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-131",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 26 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-789",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "LADDERS ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-132",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1. Portable Ladders. A ladder that can readily be moved or carried, and usually consists of side rails  joined at intervals by steps, rungs, or cleats (29 CFR 1910.21, Definitions). Figure 26.1. illustrates portable  ladder positioning requirements.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-790",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.1. Requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-791",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.1.1. Ladder side rails must extend at least 3 feet above the upper landing surface for access. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-792",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.1.2. While not mandatory, it's recommended to use fall protection with portable ladders. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-793",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.1.3. The ladder should maintain a 4:1 pitch, meaning for every 4 feet vertically, the ladder  should be 1 foot away from the structure. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-794",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.1.4. Always maintain three points of contact while climbing or descending the ladder. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-795",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.2. Inspection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-796",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.2.1. Check the ladder's load rating and ensure it's not exceeded. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-797",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.2.2. Before each use, inspect the ladder. Check for tight joints, straight rungs, and intact side  rails. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-798",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.2.3. Metal components must be free from corrosion, with all bolts and rivets securely in place. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-799",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.2.4. Ensure the ladder is appropriate for the intended task, such as using fiberglass ladders for  electrical work. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-800",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.2.5. For extension ladders, ensure movable parts operate smoothly, ropes are intact, and all  components are in good condition. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-801",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.3. Hazards. Common hazards include missing hardware, improper ladder setup, standing on the  ladder's top, and using the wrong ladder for the job. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-802",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.1.4. The NIOSH and CDC offer a "ladder safety" app to aid workers and inspectors in measuring  ladder angles. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-133",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2. Fixed Ladders. A ladder with rails or individual rungs that is permanently attached to a structure,  building, or equipment. Fixed ladders include individual-rung ladders, but not ship stairs, step bolts, or  manhole steps (29 CFR 1910.21, Definitions). Figure 26.1. gives an example of a fixed ladder.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-803",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2.1. Requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-804",
          "pageNumber": 57,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2.1.1. Refer to 29 CFR 1910.28(b)(9)(i) for detailed fixed ladder requirements, which vary  depending on the ladder's installation date and accompanying safety equipment. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 57,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p-805",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2.1.2. Depending on when the ladder was installed, it may require a personal fall arrest system,  ladder safety system, cage, or well. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-806",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2.2. Application. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-807",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2.2.1. Determine the ladder's installation date to ensure compliance with the appropriate fall  protection regulations. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-808",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2.2.2. Inspect fixed ladders for environmental damage, rust, sharp edges, and compliance with  safety standards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-809",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.2.3. Hazards. Common hazards include corrosion, rust, sharp edges, and non-compliance with safety  regulations, leading to ladder disrepair. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-134",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3. Ladder Safety Systems. A system designed to eliminate or reduce the possibility of falling from a  ladder. A ladder safety system usually consists of a carrier, safety sleeve, lanyard, connectors, and body  harness. Cages and wells are not ladder safety systems (29 CFR 1910.21, Definitions). Figure 26.1. gives  examples of ladder safety systems.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-810",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3.1. Requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-811",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3.1.1. Each ladder safety system enables climbing with both hands free and without continuously  holding, pushing, or pulling any part of the system. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-812",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3.1.2. Systems may be cable or fixed rail types, permanently attached to the ladder or supporting  structure. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-813",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3.2. Application. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-814",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3.2.1. Inspect fixed ladders to determine if they require ladder safety systems, especially if they  are 24 feet or higher. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-815",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3.2.2. Check ladder components for signs of damage, corrosion, or rust. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-816",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "26.3.3. Hazards. Hazards include damaged components, corrosion, and failure to install safety systems  despite regulatory requirements. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-817",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Figure 26.1. Ladder Specifications ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "table-14",
          "pageNumber": 58,
          "rows": [
            {
              "cells": [
                {
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "id": "p-818",
                      "pageNumber": 58,
                      "text": [
                        {
                          "text": "Portable Ladder Positioning Requirements",
                        },
                      ],
                      "type": "paragraph",
                    },
                  ],
                  "colSpan": 1,
                  "id": "th-89",
                  "isHeader": true,
                  "pageNumber": 58,
