      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 58,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-135",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 27 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-821",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "LEADING EDGES  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-136",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.1. Definition. Leading edge means the edge of a floor, roof, or formwork for a floor or other  walking/working surface (such as the deck) which changes location as additional floor, roof, decking, or  formwork sections are placed, formed, or constructed. A leading edge is considered to be an "unprotected  side and edge" during periods when it is not actively and continuously under construction (29 CFR  1926.500(b), Definitions). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-137",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.2. Requirements. IAW 29 CFR 1926.501(b)(2): ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-822",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.2.1. If workers are working on a leading edge that's 6 feet (1.8 meters) or higher above lower levels,  they must be protected from falling. This can be done using guardrail systems, safety net systems, or  personal fall arrest systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-823",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.2.2.1. If the employer can show that using these systems is not practical or would make the  situation more dangerous, they must create and follow a fall protection plan that meets the  requirements outlined in 29 CFR 1926.502(k). ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-824",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.2.2.2. Note: There's an assumption that using at least one of the fall protection systems  mentioned above is feasible and doesn't increase the risk. So, the employer needs to prove that  using a fall protection plan complying with 29 CFR 1926.502(k) is more suitable for a specific  workplace situation instead of using any of those systems. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-825",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.2.2. If workers are on a surface that's 6 feet (1.8 meters) or higher above a lower level and leading  edges are being constructed nearby, but they're not directly involved in the leading-edge work, they  still need protection from falling.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-826",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.2.2.1. This can be provided by a guardrail system, safety net system, or PFAS.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-827",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.2.2.2. If a guardrail system is chosen and a controlled access zone has been set up for leading  edge work, the control line can be used instead of a guardrail along the edge that runs parallel to  the leading edge. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-138",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.3. Hazards. Common hazards are workers not following written program requirements, unauthorized  workers in the leading-edge area, and the area is improperly marked off.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-828",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Note: Leading edge work is usually contracted out. Air Force and Space Force Safety professionals will  rarely deal with leading edge work operations. Research the scope of work and verify standards before  consulting on these types of operations. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-139",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "27.4. Visual Aids. The visual aids include demonstrations of drop tests for Leading Edge and Non-Leading  Edge PFAS. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-829",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57513",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Leading Edge PFAS Drop Test",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-830",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57513) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-831",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57511",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "Non-Leading Edge PFAS Drop Test",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-832",
          "pageNumber": 59,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.milsuite.mil/video/watch/video/57511)   ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 59,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-140",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 28 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-833",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "STEEL ERECTING ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-141",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.1. Overview. In steel erection, a new, very narrow working surface is constantly being created as skeletal  steel is erected at various heights. For many steel erectors, especially connectors, the work starts at the top  level of the structure. This means that anchor points above foot level are often limited or unavailable.  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-142",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2. Requirements. IAW 29 CFR 1926.760(a):  ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-834",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2.1. Employees who are on a walking/working surface with an unprotected edge more than 15 feet  above a lower level must be protected by conventional fall protection. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-835",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2.2. Perimeter safety cables must be installed at the final interior and exterior perimeters of multi- story structures as soon as the decking has been installed. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-836",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2.3. Connectors and employees working in controlled decking zones must be protected from fall  hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-837",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2.4. Install perimeter safety cables on multi-story structures after metal decking installation. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-838",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2.5. Protect connectors and workers in controlled decking zones from fall hazards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-839",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2.6. Connectors must be protected from falls over two stories or 30 feet, complete connector training,  and use fall protection systems above 15 feet. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-840",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.2.7. Establish Controlled Decking Zone (CDZ) for initial metal decking installation, ensuring worker  safety and limited access. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-143",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "28.3. Visual Aids. The visual aid is a link to an OSHA eTool that helps educates employers and workers  on the revised steel erection standards. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-841",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Visual Aid. ",
            },
            {
              "link": {
                "href": "https://www.osha.gov/etools/steel-erection",
                "kind": "external",
              },
              "text": "OSHA Steel Erecting General Requirements",
            },
            {
              "text": " ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-842",
          "pageNumber": 60,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "(https://www.osha.gov/etools/steel-erection) ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 60,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "h-144",
          "level": 1,
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "Chapter 29 ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-843",
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "FALL PROTECTION PPE MAINTENANCE AND STORAGE ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "h-145",
          "level": 2,
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "29.1. Best Practices for Equipment Storage. This section outlines essential guidelines for storing fall  protection equipment safely and effectively. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "heading",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-844",
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "29.1.1. Safe Storage Practices: It is imperative to store fall protection equipment according to the  manufacturer's guidelines. This not only ensures the equipment's longevity but also guarantees its safe  use. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-845",
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "29.1.2. Tagging for Maintenance: Equipment requiring repairs or maintenance should be clearly  tagged with an AFTO Form 244, Industrial Support Equipment Record, and removed from service  until rectified. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-846",
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "29.1.3. Inspection Before Use: Equipment stored for an extended period should undergo inspection by  a competent person before being used to ensure its integrity and functionality. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-847",
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "29.1.4. Proper Storage Conditions: When not in use, fall protection gear should be hung in a cool, dry  place. Considerations for storage include avoiding contact with the ground or dirty environments,  moisture, direct sunlight, chemical exposure, sharp objects, abrasive surfaces, and extreme  temperatures. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
        {
          "id": "p-848",
          "pageNumber": 61,
          "text": [
            {
              "text": "29.1.5. Improper Storage Condition: Common pitfalls to avoid while storing PPE, include exposure  to moisture, sunlight, chemicals, sharp objects, and extreme temperatures. Note: Common inspection  findings identified during equipment inspections that necessitate immediate action include fabric  deterioration, metal corrosion, webbing damage, and issues with hooks or gates. ",
            },
          ],
          "type": "paragraph",
        },
      ],
      "height": 792,
      "pageNumber": 61,
      "warnings": [],
      "width": 612,
    },
    {
      "blocks": [
        {
